333,0.44001828965495830797785522825102193725,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000000000,22.91022933333333333333333333333333333352,8.3639\\r\\noutcome_compressed_margin_0.28,64,holding,5,9,costs_before_remaining_receipts,116.6863231841558859636799020757147457632,-6.203463491379131923314879356875851614375,0,0,0,0,0,110.4828596927767540403650227188388941488,-0.000000000000000000000000000000000000025,0.3866666666666666666666666666666666666667,0.2329310000000000000000000000000000000000,1.183850583333333333333333333333333333333,0.36668190804579858998154602354251828104,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000000000,23.13189600000000000000000000000000000019,8.3639\\r\\noutcome_compressed_margin_0.28,64,holding,5,9,remaining_receipts,110.4828596927767540403650227188388941488,9.166666666666666666666666666666666666665,0,0,0,0,0,119.6495263594434207070316893855055608155,0.000000000000000000000000000000000000035,0.3866666666666666666666666666666666666667,0.2329310000000000000000000000000000000000,1.183850583333333333333333333333333333333,0.36668190804579858998154602354251828104,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000000000,23.13189600000000000000000000000000000019,8.3639\\r\\noutcome_compressed_margin_0.28,65,holding,5,10,costs_before_remaining_receipts,119.6495263594434207070316893855055608155,-6.142349840038165491651288352952098567535,0,0,0,0,0,113.5071765194052552153804010325534622480,0.000000000000000000000000000000000000035,0.3866666666666666666666666666666666666667,0.2329310000000000000000000000000000000000,1.183850583333333333333333333333333333333,0.30556825670483215831795501961876523420,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000000000,23.35356266666666666666666666666666666686,8.3639\\r\\noutcome_compressed_margin_0.28,65,holding,5,10,remaining_receipts,113.5071765194052552153804010325534622480,9.166666666666666666666666666666666666665,0,0,0,0,0,122.6738431860719218820470676992201289147,0.000000000000000000000000000000000000035,0.3866666666666666666666666666666666666667,0.2329310000000000000000000000000000000000,1.183850583333333333333333333333333333333,0.30556825670483215831795501961876523420,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000000000,23.35356266666666666666666666666666666686,8.3639\\r\\noutcome_compressed_margin_0.28,66,holding,5,11,costs_before_remaining_receipts,122.6738431860719218820470676992201289147,-6.091421797254026798598295849682304361835,0,0,0,0,0,116.5824213888178950834487718495378245529,0.000000000000000000000000000000000000035,0.3866666666666666666666666666666666666667,0.2329310000000000000000000000000000000000,1.183850583333333333333333333333333333333,0.25464021392069346526496251634897102850,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000000000,23.57522933333333333333333333333333333353,8.3639\\r\\noutcome_compressed_margin_0.28,66,holding,5,11,remaining_receipts,116.5824213888178950834487718495378245529,9.166666666666666666666666666666666666665,0,0,0,0,0,125.7490880554845617501154385162044912196,0.000000000000000000000000000000000000035,0.3866666666666666666666666666666666666667,0.2329310000000000000000000000000000000000,1.183850583333333333333333333333333333333,0.25464021392069346526496251634897102850,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000000000,23.57522933333333333333333333333333333353,8.3639\\r\\noutcome_compressed_margin_0.28,67,holding,5,12,costs_before_remaining_receipts,125.7490880554845617501154385162044912196,-6.048981761600577887720802096957475857075,0,0,0,0,0,119.7001062938839838623946364192470153625,-0.000000000000000000000000000000000000025,0.3866666666666666666666666666666666666667,0.2329310000000000000000000000000000000000,1.183850583333333333333333333333333333333,0.21220017826724455438746876362414252374,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000000000,23.79689600000000000000000000000000000020,8.3639\\r\\noutcome_compressed_margin_0.28,67,holding,5,12,remaining_receipts,119.7001062938839838623946364192470153625,9.166666666666666666666666666666666666665,0,0,0,0,0,128.8667729605506505290613030859136820292,0.000000000000000000000000000000000000035,0.3866666666666666666666666666666666666667,0.2329310000000000000000000000000000000000,1.183850583333333333333333333333333333333,0.21220017826724455438746876362414252374,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000000000,23.79689600000000000000000000000000000020,8.3639\\r\\n\"","\"# Rajputana Biodiesel Limited\\n## SME research | v3.0 | Information cutoff 20 October 2026\\n\\nAll currency amounts are INR crore unless explicitly stated. F = sourced fact; A = analyst assumption; I = inference. NS means not scoreable, ND not disclosed/established, NM not meaningful and NA not applicable. This is research, not an instruction to transact.\\n\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**Hold — unresolved. A working-capital-intensive biodiesel growth situation, not an evidence-backed compounder.** Rajputana has expanded operating sales and parent-attributable earnings, with two biodiesel sites and repeat oil-marketing-company allocations. The decisive constraint is cash: every selected historical year has negative CFO, FY26 reported CFO benefited from a large FD release, and growth absorbed inventory, advances and new debt. Reported growth cannot be cleanly separated into organic volume, realization, trading and acquired scope. A headline earnings multiple does not compensate for an unverified current funding bridge. BQS, GQS and IAS therefore remain **NS**, not low-score substitutes; no investable ranking or ordinary expected return is assigned. Reconsider only after current liquidity/renewals, comparable product economics and incremental-capital evidence are established. Even hypothetical rescue returns do not make optional finance committed.\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n| Item | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Issuer | Rajputana Biodiesel Limited / RAJPUTANA / INE0VHU01019 |\\n| Cutoff / quote | 2026-10-20 / 2026-10-19 |\\n| Price / shares / market cap | ₹207.70 / 7033500 / ₹146.0858 crore |\\n| Horizon / equity hurdle | 5 years / 15%; sensitivity12% and18% |\\n| Rubric / scope | v3.0 ordinary nonfinancial; SME forensic overlay; consolidated AS, parent-shareholder claims |\\n| Reference position | ₹5 lakh, not a suitability recommendation |\\n\\nF: Exact issuer identity is matched by ISIN in the official October19 NSE security master and daily tape, not by a similarly named biodiesel company. Incorporated10November2016; public-company conversion8July2024; SME listing3December2024. Current issued shares are70.335lakh, face value₹10, authorized80lakh. The financial record follows consolidated AS accounts and separately identifies parent claims and minority interests. Latest fully audited fiscal year is31March2026. Subsequent announcements only through the20October cutoff are used, despite research retrieval on23October. Later prices and postcutoff news are excluded.\\n\\n**Confidence:** High on FY25/FY26 audited primary P&L and current observed tape; Medium on balance-sheet classification, historical comparisons and capital allocation; Low on organic demand, mature incremental returns, actual October cash and refinancing. March2026 shareholding is verified but not relabeled as October. A qualified research hold is not an allegation of fraud or an assertion of current default.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Finding / resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 | Conditional | No substantiated severe integrity failure in audits reviewed; demand RPT recoverability and cash/FD availability require clarification. Obtain repayment schedules, confirmations, recoverability and board approvals for material related-party loans. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Latest audit is unmodified, but historical restatement/AR comparative differences and overview EBITDA versus P&L are material to a long-run incremental-return claim; no unfavorable allegation is inferred. Reconcile IPO restated FY24 capital/cashflow to later comparative, obtain consistent parent-attributable EPS and organic volume/price bridge. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | No proven default. Actual October cash, restrictions, headroom and renewals are not established; diagnostic pre-entry stress breaches without optional finance. Current bank statements, facility utilization/renewals, monthly collections, subsidiary fungibility and fully funded capex commitments. |\\n| G4 | Conditional | Observed trading and current lot established; order-book spread, circuits, current surveillance and execution feasibility remain unverified. Exchange restriction checks and executable quote/depth at time of proposed trade; do not treat master market-maker field as bid-ask spread. |\\n\\nF: March31 shareholder filing records six promoters holding46,26,000 shares(65.77%), public24,07,500(34.23%),808 holders and14,06,700 locked shares(20%). It declares no promoter pledge/other encumbrance, convertibles, warrants or outstanding ESOP as of that date. No September2026 primary filing was established. Shareholder identifiers are deliberately excluded from the data package. [SHP26 pp1–3]\\n\\n### Implementation and liquidity\\n\\n| Sessions | Mean ₹ | Median ₹ | Absent/zero | Days at2% | Days at5% | Days at10% |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 20 | 1425105.00 | 603062.50 | 4 | 41.455 | 16.582 | 8.291 |\\n| 60 | 1752649.17 | 670550.00 | 6 | 37.283 | 14.913 | 7.457 |\\n\\nF/A: The sample is60 exchange sessions through19October, not60 issuer trading observations. An absent issuer row is assigned zero capacity for the conservative liquidity calculation, not described as an observed zero-price bar. One500-share lot costs₹1,03,850. Four lots cost₹4,15,400; five exceed the₹5lakh reference. The60-session median-based starting L is3; lot indivisibility, absent sessions and unavailable spread/depth reduce it explicitly to2. At5% participation the median-day rupee allowance is smaller than one lot, so the fractional-day formula is not an executable sale schedule. No actual bid-ask spread is inferred from the security-master market-maker field. Verify live surveillance, circuit limits, quotes and lot rules before implementation. The reference amount is not a recommended allocation.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\n| Composite | Known contribution | Coverage | Raw missing-data bound | Cap | Final |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | 2.940 | 76.00% | 3.180–5.340 | None | NS |\\n| GQS | 1.950 | 45.00% | 2.500–7.450 | 5 | NS |\\n| IAS | NS | 30.00% | 1.845–6.435 | None | NS |\\n\\nAll weights remain original. Missing factors are not assigned5, not omitted from denominators and not renormalized. Bounds are mathematical missing-data bounds, not confidence intervals. GQS is capped at5 because CASH2 reflects persistent poor conversion; the final result remains NS. IAS uses nested BQS bounds, not a loose1–10 quality substitution. The literal IAS missing-factor bound is1.30–7.60 with Q/R/P NS; the table shows tighter nested propagation of the BQS evidence. Formal IAS coverage counts its original scoreable components; underlying weighted evidence coverage is also retained in canonical data. G1/G3 do not receive numeric failure caps because an evidenced severe integrity/funding failure has not been established. G2/G3 hold status independently prevents a favorable investment conclusion.\\n\\nBQS\\n| Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 0.20 | 5 | 1.000 |\\n| MOAT | 0.20 | 3 | 0.600 |\\n| REV | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| DUR | 0.10 | 5 | 0.500 |\\n| CAP | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| CASH | 0.10 | 2 | 0.200 |\\n| BAL | 0.08 | 3 | 0.240 |\\n| EXEC | 0.08 | 5 | 0.400 |\\n\\nGQS\\n| Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| REV | 0.40 | NS | NS |\\n| DUR | 0.20 | 5 | 1.000 |\\n| EARN | 0.15 | 5 | 0.750 |\\n| CAP | 0.15 | NS | NS |\\n| CASH | 0.10 | 2 | 0.200 |\\n\\nIAS\\n| Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS_final | .25 | NS | NS |\\n| R | .35 | NS | NS |\\n| D | .20 | 2 | 0.400 |\\n| P | .10 | NS | NS |\\n| L | .10 | 2 | 0.200 |\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence / contrary |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 5 | Medium | AR26 auditors report no identified fraud, defaults or statutory auditor resignation and an unmodified opinion. Related-party advances of 1.0378 crore to Deepprabha remain, and demand loans lack contractual repayment schedules; IPO proceeds fully deployed, including 12.20 crore working capital. Contrary: No substantiated diversion or minority abuse was established; related-party commercial dealings and short-duration director funding are disclosed. Demand-loan ambiguity warrants evidence, not a fraud allegation. [AR26 pp136–143,170–184] |\\n| MOAT | 3 | Medium | IPO risk discussion explicitly describes low entry barriers; customers are competitive OMC tenders rather than protected distribution. Spot feedstock purchases have no long-term supplier contracts, while top-ten customers accounted for 85.98% in FY24 and 100% in the July2024 stub. Contrary: Multi-feedstock process capability, BIS-qualified supply and repeat OMC allocations are useful operating capabilities, but durable price power or structurally superior cash returns is not demonstrated. [IPO pp29–47,96–109; CALL25] |\\n| REV | NS | Low for underlying demand; High for latest reported revenue | Reported FY23–26 revenue grows from 23.4085 to122.0292 crore, but March2024 acquisition makes the window non-constant scope. FY25–26 revenue grows81.29%, but audited annual volume/ASP and external intercompany elimination bridge do not establish organic demand separately from feedstock/pricing and trading. Contrary: Physical IPO production and FY26 parent/subsidiary sales confirm material scaling; neither a large reported CAGR nor capacity arithmetic supplies the missing comparable organic endpoint. [IPO F50–58 and p153; AR25; AR26 pp144–146; PRES26] |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | Existing group biodiesel capacity is stated as170KLPD across Phulera and Meerut, with repeat OMC allocations. An October6 CBG filing increases project registration to18TPD but still requires establishment approvals; no commissioned CBG earnings or binding subsidy proceeds are verified. Contrary: Renewable-fuel policy and byproducts provide plausible demand access; feedstock availability, tender price, dispatch and collection determine accessible economics, not national blending targets. [PRES26; CBG26; LOI26; CALL25] |\\n| CAP | NS | Low for incremental cohort economics | FY26 cash capex11.8497 crore and operating working-capital absorption are substantial, with8.3639 crore CWIP still uncommissioned. The three-year window spans acquisition, restatement and the1.0276 crore noncash goodwill/securities-premium reclassification; a clean organic/acquired invested-capital and mature-cohort bridge is unavailable. Contrary: FY26 normalized operating profit improves and a one-year accounting incremental proxy can be calculated. It does not establish returns on new CBG/land or distinguish improved utilization of old plants from new capital productivity. [AR26 pp144–166; AR25 goodwill rollforward; IPO F56–58] |\\n| CASH | 2 | High | CFO is negative for every FY22–26 year in the selected primary series; profitable FY24–26 CFO totals negative22.9126 crore. FY26 CFO negative2.8470 includes4.6488 crore FD release; removing that financial-asset release gives analytical CFO negative7.4958, while cash capex is11.8497. Contrary: FY26 receivables grow much less than revenue and FY25 CFO deterioration partly reflects deposits, but repeated inventory/advances absorption and aged balances prevent treating the issue as one transitory build. [AR26 pp146,160–166; AR25; IPO F52] |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | Normalized gross debt28.3579 crore includes23.0980 crore positive working-capital facilities; reported cash2.0582 includes1.3982 physical cash. No undrawn facilities are disclosed, while capital creditors1.9551, tax provision1.8855 and term maturities1.0854 require cash; all modeled no-new-funding branches breach in the diagnostic pre-entry bridge. Contrary: Auditors report no default, term debt amortization is modest and EBITDA covers recorded interest. The stress is not proof of actual default or lender withdrawal; actual October cash and renewals remain unresolved. [AR26 pp144–146,150–173; model_inputs.json] |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | Group manufacturing revenue has scaled after the Nirvaanraj acquisition, and both operating biodiesel sites are identified at170KLPD combined. The Nov2025 CBG/pellet diversification timetable is not demonstrated as delivered by cutoff; October2026 document remains a registration/approval-stage announcement. Contrary: Acquisition integration and new OMC awards are tangible progress, but announcements, allocated tenders and carbon-credit issuance do not establish commissioned project cash returns. [CALL25; PRES26; CBG26; AR26] |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium | FY26 parent-attributable PAT9.0623 exceeds FY25 restated comparative5.3663; clean operating EBITDA15.0556 excludes1.0598 other income. FY26 group PAT10.5191 includes1.4568 attributable to minority holders; using the group headline or FY25 old pre-NCI EPS would overstate shareholder earnings. Contrary: Operating growth is real, not wholly subsidy-driven; FY25 subsidy0.90 crore, FY26 subsidy0.15 and inconsistent overview EBITDA require normalization. CASH is scored separately, not double-counted here. [AR26 pp144–146,162–166; AR25] |\\n| R | NS | Low | Every unfinanced path stops at its first pre-entry cash breach and generates no ordinary IRR. Conditional rescue returns rely on uncommitted rollover, incremental loan and discounted equity; those cannot be substituted for a supported base-case investment return. Contrary: The bounded diagnostic can generate conditional prices and returns; numerical availability does not establish financing or current cash. [model_inputs.json; model_engine.py] |\\n| D | 2 | Medium | A linked feedstock-margin and working-capital stress requires incremental capital despite reported accounting profit. The bounded rescue bear branch still breaches before entry; the shareholder recovery diagnostic is severely exposed to debt and inventory/advance haircuts, rather than being assigned an ordinary post-breach IRR. Contrary: There is operating plant value and profitable demand, with no reported default. The low score represents a supported loss path, not a forecast of inevitable failure. [AR26; model_inputs.json; model_engine.py] |\\n| P | NS | Low | Reverse operating requirements are computed at the current price, but are conditional on cash availability and financing that are not established. CBG, carbon credits and future subsidies are excluded rather than used to justify today's valuation. Contrary: Reported parent earnings imply a moderate headline PE. A low headline multiple alone cannot establish undemanding enterprise expectations when cash funding is unresolved. [AR26; CBG26; model_engine.py] |\\n| L | 2 | Medium | Conservative60-session median gives14.913 days at5% participation for a5-lakh reference position, starting score3. Current official lot500 costs103850 rupees, exceeding5% of median daily value; six absent/no-trade sessions and unavailable bid-ask depth justify one-point downgrade. Contrary: The security did trade on October19 and mean turnover is higher than median; no suspension is inferred. Current surveillance/circuit/quote depth still needs live execution verification. [market_extract.json; security_extract.json] |\\n\\nJudgment sensitivity: changing GOV or EXEC by one point moves BQS bounds by0.20 or0.08 respectively, and IAS bounds by0.05 or0.02 through its25% BQS weight; GQS is unchanged. These small judgment effects do not resolve REV/CAP/R/P missingness or the gates. Conventional half-up rounding is used only for display; Decimal arithmetic and unrounded classification inputs are retained.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nF: The core activity converts non-edible oils, used cooking oil and other suitable feedstocks into biodiesel, with glycerine and fatty-acid byproducts. Parent Phulera capacity is stated at90KLPD and subsidiary Meerut at80KLPD. Nirvaanraj Energy was acquired28March2024 and is75.21% owned. FY26 parent sales₹59.0950 plus subsidiary sales₹69.1068 exceed group external sales₹122.0292 by₹6.1726 of eliminations. This prevents adding standalone and subsidiary turnover as if both were external. [AR26; PRES26]\\n\\nF/I: Customers include Indian Oil, Bharat Petroleum and Hindustan Petroleum through tender allocations. Tender quantity is not cash: pricing, call-offs, dispatch, product acceptance, credit and collection matter. No complete current order-to-dispatch-to-receipt rollforward was located, so order announcements are not capitalized as guaranteed backlog. Sales to a large state-owned customer reduce neither supplier pricing risk nor all collection/acceptance risk. The company also has byproduct and trading activity, making total revenue an imperfect physical production denominator. [IPO pp96–109; CALL25; LOI26]\\n\\nF: Agro LLP,99% owned, formed16March2026, has no FY26 turnover and is an agriculture/land-related early-stage investment. Arvant Bioenergy was acquired25August2026 for₹10,000 with nil reported turnover. Neither is assigned operating earnings or a premium simply because it may support future bioenergy expansion. [AR26 subsidiary schedule; ACQ26; ACQDONE26]\\n\\nA/I: At170KLPD ×300 assumed operating days ×1,000litres/KL ×₹80/litre, a rough biodiesel revenue capacity is₹408crore. This is a **unit arithmetic ceiling proxy**, not audited output, an addressable-market estimate or a forecast. The₹80 realization comes from a dated management discussion, not a guaranteed current contract. Product yield, density, grade mix, maintenance downtime and OMC orders can all lower realizable sales. No CBG or carbon-credit revenue is embedded in this ceiling or base valuation.\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\nF/I: Feedstock flexibility, conversion knowledge, acceptable specifications and repeat tender participation are useful but contestable capabilities. The IPO itself describes low entry barriers. Supplier concentration and spot purchasing without long-term supply contracts limit cost protection. There is no verified automatic pass-through or cost-plus contract that guarantees recovery of feedstock inflation; tender realizations can reset on a different timetable from raw-material purchases. A3percentage-point margin stress is therefore a cashflow scenario, not a macro slogan. [IPO pp29–47,96–109; CALL25]\\n\\nF: Top-ten customers were79.64% inFY22,89.72% inFY23,85.98% inFY24 and100% in the July2024 stub; the largest FY24 customer was26.16%, rising to39.25% in the stub. Top-ten supplier concentration rose to83.68% inFY24 and92.74% in the stub; the largest FY24 supplier was38.90%. These are **dated disclosures**, not claimed current concentration. Current FY26 customer/supplier breakdown and independent feedstock-cost advantage remain ND. [IPO concentration tables]\\n\\n| Operating comparator | Why relevant / limitation | Primary source |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Kotyark Industries | Closest listed biodiesel/glycerine tender/feedstock comparator; no unverified peer multiple used. Undated product description used only for business overlap. | [Source](https://www.kotyark.com/biodisel) |\\n| Universal Biofuels (Aemetis subsidiary) | India biodiesel/refined glycerin operator, competing OMC allocation model; February2025 restart approval illustrates plant/regulatory execution risk. Dated24February2025. US parent valuation not comparable. | [Source](https://aemetis.com/aemetis-india-plant-approved-by-government-to-restart-biodiesel-production/) |\\n| Emami Agrotech | Integrated edible-oil/biodiesel producer offers potentially stronger feedstock integration; unlisted, no clean comparable enterprise multiple. Business overlap only; current undated capacity and largest-producer marketing not imported into cutoff valuation. | [Source](https://www.emamigroup.com/group-of-companies/agrotech/bio-diesel-edible-oils-and-foods/) |\\n\\nI: These are operating comparisons, not a hand-picked valuation-multiple set. Kotyark is the closest listed biodiesel/glycerine analogue; Aemetis' US parent includes other businesses and financial risks; Emami is unlisted and integrated with edible oils. Their presence refutes an assumption of an uncontested market, but their promotional scale claims do not establish Rajputana's market share. No peer PE premium is imported into terminal value. No verified switching-cost, brand, network or patent mechanism supports a high moat score.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\n| Period | Start revenue | End revenue | Reported CAGR | Basis |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY23–FY26 | 23.4085 | 122.0292 | 73.39% | Non-constant scope; not scored |\\n| FY21–FY26 context only | 9.6900 | 122.0292 | 65.97% | FY21 MD&A context not restated, FY24 acquisition and price mix; not scored |\\n\\n| Year | Revenue addition | Reported YoY |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| FY2022 | NS | NS |\\n| FY2023 | 6.3364 | 37.12% |\\n| FY2024 | 30.0512 | 128.38% |\\n| FY2025 | 13.8534 | 25.91% |\\n| FY2026 | 54.7161 | 81.29% |\\n\\nF/I: FY26 growth is substantial, but the preferred five-year comparable organic window is unavailable. A FY21 revenue endpoint appears in IPO MD&A at₹9.6900crore with other income₹0.0811 and total₹9.7711; the early directors comparative gives total income₹9.7669, and nearby narrative year labels are inconsistent. It is retained as context rather than asserted to be a clean restated series. A three-year FY23–26 window is also acquisition-distorted because Nirvaanraj entered only near FY24 year-end. The numerical schedule would start at9 for a comparable CAGR above30%, but the basis required to apply that schedule is not established. REV is therefore NS, with no trajectory uplift or arbitrary small-base penalty. [IPO p153,F50–58; AR25; AR26]\\n\\nF: IPO parent production was1,908.61KL inFY22 and2,525.38KL inFY23. Later windows of4,355.33KL forApril2023–January2024 and4,246.01KL forFebruary–July2024 overlap fiscal boundaries and cannot be concatenated as annualFY24/FY25 volumes. Reported utilization figures refer to their own changing capacity windows. FY25 andFY26 audited annual volume/ASP/yield bridges were not established. Standalone FY25 sales fell from the FY24 presentation while consolidated sales rose; FY26 parent growth resumes, confirming that acquisition scope and allocation across entities matter. A claimed capacity increase is not evidence of organic volume growth without dispatch data.\\n\\nI: What is known is higher group revenue and operating profit. What is not known is how much came from external liters, traded material, byproduct mix, feedstock price inflation or stable-margin genuine demand. Future revenue assumptions in the model are explicitly analyst paths and never feed back into historical REV scoring.\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nF/I: Existing plants provide a plausible production runway, but incremental orders consume feedstock cash, finished inventory and tax credits before shareholder cash. Reported FY26 inventory₹35.7372 includes₹28.4544 finished goods and₹7.2828 raw material. GST ITC₹7.0476 is operating capital, not freely distributable cash. A national blending goal does not prove that Rajputana can source feedstock, win profitable tenders and finance inventory at the required scale. [AR26 notes16–20]\\n\\nA: The cash model uses annual sales paths bear110/105/110/115/120, base140/160/180/200/220 and bull160/200/250/300/350. Margins are7%→8%,12% flat and13%→14%, respectively. No policy subsidy, uncommissioned CBG, carbon-credit sale or Arvant earnings is used to balance cash. Working capital targets differ by case and move gradually, not via a free instant release. The bull requires materially better inventory and receivable efficiency than FY26; it is not a conservative extrapolation.\\n\\nA/I: Productive fixed assets roll forward by funded capex less depreciation. Each bucket must fund at least₹0.12 of incremental productive capital per₹1 of annual sales aboveFY26, in addition to maintenance. Existing8.3639 of CWIP remains committed capital historically but stranded for modeled earnings and terminal recovery. Sensitivity raises incremental capital intensity to0.20 and separately adds10crore unproductive project capex. This does not claim an engineering-validated plant budget: credible EPC, yields and commissioning economics remain missing.\\n\\nF/I: The6October2026 notice increases CBG project **registration** from5TPD to18TPD and states that mandatory establishment approvals will be sought. It is not commissioning, an executed EPC cost guarantee, a proven feedstock supply contract or a subsidy cash receipt. Management's earlier high CBG margin commentary is neither verified unit economics nor a reason to borrow a renewable-sector valuation multiple. CAP remains NS despite the calculable one-year accounting proxy below; GQS remains NS and capped at5 for poor cash realization.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\nF: The audited record discloses no reported fraud, default, willful-defaulter status or statutory auditor resignation forFY26, and the opinion is unmodified. The August announcement's auditor wording concerns a secretarial appointment, not evidence of a statutory auditor exit. Parent internal financial controls received an unmodified opinion; subsidiary applicability/exemptions mean that cannot be inflated into a fully tested group-wide controls claim. [AR26 standalone/consolidated audit,CARO,IFC; ACQ26]\\n\\nF/I: Related-party demand loans lack fixed repayment schedules, limiting the auditor's ability to comment on regular repayment. External Deepprabha advances stand at₹1.0378crore; disclosed subsidiary loans eliminate within the group and must not be counted again as an external asset. A disclosed related-party supplier purchase of₹6.9636crore including GST and temporary director borrowings deserve ordinary approval/terms/recoverability review, not automatic fraud language. Modest disclosed director remuneration does not neutralize capital-allocation or cash-access questions. [AR26 related-party and CARO notes]\\n\\nF: IPO proceeds were reported fully utilized:₹4.1873 subsidiary loan,₹12.20 working capital,₹5.8604 general corporate purposes and₹2.4523 issue expenses, totaling₹24.70crore. The fact that IPO working capital has been deployed does not imply a remaining funding buffer. The small₹0.0022 IPO account balance pending reimbursement is excluded from modeled available cash. No ordinary dividends are assumed. [AR26 note41]\\n\\n| Date | Claim | Delivery assessment | Source |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2025-11-18 | Improve utilization and commission diversification within following year; high CBG margin guidance | Biodiesel scaling supported; unit-volume utilization bridge absent. October6 CBG document only registration and approvals pending, no commissioning/cash proof. | CALL25;CBG26 |\\n| 2026-05-27 | 170KLPD biodiesel capacity and circular-business opportunity | Capacity claim retained as reported capacity, not production or normalized economic return. Carbon-credit cash receipts not verified. | PRES26;AR26 |\\n| 2026-08-22 | Acquire Arvant | CompletedAugust25 for10000rupees; zero-turnover vehicle not a cash-generating acquisition. | ACQ26;ACQDONE26 |\\n\\nI: Execution is mixed: operating plants and reported revenue have scaled, but diversification and cash realization have not been proven on the same timetable. No claim is made that registration-stage CBG is already overdue against a binding legal completion date. Monitoring should distinguish changed guidance, a credible revised funded plan and actual commissioned output.\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\n### Audited historical series and per-share perimeter\\n\\n| FY | Revenue | Op EBITDA | Group PAT | Parent PAT | CFO | Cash capex | FCF | Debt | Cash |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY2022 | 17.0721 | 1.2602 | 0.1997 | 0.1997 | -0.5277 | 0.3762 | -0.9039 | 10.9361 | 0.1675 |\\n| FY2023 | 23.4085 | 3.5086 | 1.6883 | 1.6883 | -1.1655 | 0.1273 | -1.2928 | 8.8712 | 0.7623 |\\n| FY2024 | 53.4597 | 7.5413 | 4.5617 | NS | -4.7375 | 0.9452 | -5.6827 | 19.1419 | 3.7185 |\\n| FY2025 | 67.3131 | 8.9096 | 5.9199 | 5.3663 | -15.3281 | 1.8606 | -17.1887 | 14.0268 | 1.5615 |\\n| FY2026 | 122.0292 | 15.0556 | 10.5191 | 9.0623 | -2.8470 | 11.8497 | -14.6967 | 28.1438 | 2.0582 |\\n\\n| FY | Inventory | Receivables | Payables | PPE | CWIP | Goodwill | FD | Owners equity | NCI |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY2022 | 2.3112 | 1.3126 | 0.6201 | 4.1932 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1337 | -1.2892 | 0.0000 |\\n| FY2023 | 5.2079 | 3.1037 | 1.3030 | 3.7637 | 0.0382 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 4.5499 | 0.0000 |\\n| FY2024 | 12.3960 | 12.7310 | 3.3073 | 5.5301 | 0.0000 | 1.1048 | NS | 14.2203 | 0.5826 |\\n| FY2025 | 18.8743 | 15.1997 | 2.1878 | 5.7609 | 0.7450 | 0.0772 | 8.7158 | 40.8373 | 1.1363 |\\n| FY2026 | 35.7372 | 15.6527 | 5.7236 | 8.9404 | 8.3639 | 0.0772 | 4.0670 | 49.8688 | 2.5950 |\\n\\nFY22/23 use IPO-restated historical statements; FY24 uses the later AR25 comparative, and FY25 uses the AR26 comparative. These are individually labeled source series, **not a seamless organic panel**. The alternative FY24 IPO figures are retained in canonical inputs: CFO−7.4507 versus−4.7375 in the later comparative, capex2.2327 versus0.9452, owner PAT4.5243 versus later group PAT4.5617, and materially different capital/working-capital presentation. They are not averaged. FY24 parent PAT is ND in the selected later comparative, so EPS/ROE are not invented. FY25 goodwill falls by1.0276 through an explicit pre-acquisition securities-premium reclassification, not impairment, sale proceeds or reduced investment. [IPO F50–58; AR25; AR26]\\n\\nF: FY26 group PAT10.5191 includes minority1.4568; shareholder PAT is9.0623. FY25 latest comparative shareholder PAT5.3663 and EPS9.33 replace an earlier pre-NCI headline5.9199 and oldEPS10.29. Current-share EPS differs from weighted-share reported EPS in the IPO year; the canonical table retains the denominator. The FY26 overview EBITDA15.8157 is not the clean P&L-derived15.0556: PBT13.4204 +finance1.6437 +DA1.0513 −other income1.0598. The0.7601 difference is exposed, not silently attributed to a particular adjustment without a reconciliation. FY25 prior-period/exceptional adjustments are separately retained; model normalization does not reward them as recurring operating income.\\n\\n| FY | EBITDA margin | CFO/PAT | EBIT/interest | AR days | Inventory days | AP days | CCC | Current-share EPS |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY2022 | 7.38% | -2.64 | 0.89 | NS | NS | NS | NS | 0.28 |\\n| FY2023 | 14.99% | -0.69 | 3.52 | 34.43 | 75.59 | 17.20 | 92.82 | 2.40 |\\n| FY2024 | 14.11% | -1.04 | 6.34 | 54.06 | 74.60 | 17.74 | 110.92 | NS |\\n| FY2025 | 13.24% | -2.59 | 4.48 | 75.73 | 105.87 | 18.70 | 162.89 | 7.63 |\\n| FY2026 | 12.34% | -0.27 | 8.52 | 46.14 | 98.85 | 12.68 | 132.31 | 12.88 |\\n\\nDays use average opening/closing balances. AR days use total sales as a credit-sales proxy. Inventory COGS includes raw material, traded purchases, direct expense and inventory change; payable days use raw-material-plus-traded purchases as an explicitly imperfect credit-purchases proxy because raw-material consumption is not exact purchases. Reported CFO is before financing cash interest: AR26 adds back finance cost and classifies cash interest in CFF. Therefore CFO-minus-capex is a reported pre-interest proxy, not debt-free FCFF or cash available to equity. FY25 cash finance1.7580 differs from P&L finance1.7915; FY26 cash finance is1.6437. After cash capex and financing cash interest, FY25 is−18.9467 andFY26−16.3404; after also removing the FY26 FD release, the latter is−20.9892. The forward engine separately deducts cash interest and does not reuse reported CFO as FCFF. Normalized NOPAT uses25% tax, with no unusable loss benefit; FY25 adds back the disclosed0.0709 prior-period and0.0020 exceptional charge, while the historical EBIT/EBITDA table retains the strict reported-PBT bridge. The incremental NOPAT comparison therefore uses normalized FY25 operating EBIT8.0977, not8.0248. Maintenance capex is not equated to accounting depreciation.\\n\\n### Earnings-to-cash and financing\\n\\n| Window | CFO | PAT | CFO/PAT | Capex | FCF |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 3 years | -22.9126 | 21.0007 | -1.09 | 14.6555 | -37.5681 |\\n| 5 years | -24.6058 | 22.8887 | -1.08 | 15.1590 | -39.7648 |\\n\\nF: FY26 PBT13.4204 plus1.9575 noncash/nonoperating adjustments produces pre-WC cash15.3779. Payables add3.5358 and other liabilities/provisions1.9009; receivables absorb0.4530, inventory16.8629, other current assets1.4787 and short-term advances2.9076. Tax paid1.9594 leaves CFO−2.8470. Cash capex11.8497 is an investing outflow, not the explanation for negative CFO. Cash after reported capex is−14.6967. CFI−9.1008 and CFF12.4444, against openingcash1.5615, imply closing2.0581; reported2.0582 differs by0.0001crore source rounding. The model does not fabricate an exact match. [AR26 p146]\\n\\nF/I: FD balances fell8.7158→4.0670, a4.6488 release folded into operating-current-asset movements. Removing that financing-asset release gives analytical CFO−7.4958, alongside the original−2.8470. This is a classification adjustment, not a claim the cash did not arrive. Do not both improve CFO with the release and count the original deposits as intact cash. FY25 FD accumulation is contrary evidence to treating all its CFO outflow as recurring cash cost; the comparable FY24 FD endpoint is not established, so a two-year deposit-adjusted conversion is ND rather than fabricated. Current FDs include0.142208 explicitly pledged to a bank guarantee; availability of the remainder requires evidence. Physical cash1.3982 of totalcash2.0582 and OD credit balances warrant access/confirmation checks. A negative0.2141 CC book balance is normalized out of gross debt, not treated as negative borrowing and simultaneously as free cash.\\n\\nF: Positive working-capital facilities total23.0980, term debt5.0648 and other debt0.1951, totaling normalizedgross28.3579. Reported debt28.1438 includes the negative CC. Current term maturity is1.0854; disclosed capex creditors1.9551 and tax provision1.8855 are separate cash demands. No undrawn facility was disclosed. Auditor non-default findings and EBIT/interest cover are positives, not proof of fresh lender headroom. No undisclosed lease debt is invented; operating rental and lease-equalization provisions remain within their accounting scope. [AR26 notes6–12,36]\\n\\nF/I: Disputed receivables over3years0.6572, undisputed1–2years2.8702 and2–3years0.1915 coexist with no impairment provision. Supplier advances include0.4421 explicitly doubtful. Such labels warrant customer/supplier confirmations, subsequent realization and NRV tests, not an automatic100% write-off. Gross-margin gains supported by finished goods rather than cash collections need inventory-grade/obsolescence evidence. No material contingent liability was disclosed, but a pledged guarantee FD means 'no disclosed contingent amount' is not the same as no guarantee exposure.\\n\\n### Incremental capital and return bridge\\n\\n| FY | ONWC | IC asset method | IC funding method | Rounding residual |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY2025 | 34.4636 | 41.9463 | 41.9462 | 0.0001 |\\n| FY2026 | 57.0935 | 75.4268 | 75.4267 | 0.0001 |\\n\\n| FY25→FY26 capital / return bridge | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| net_cash_capex | 10.798400 |\\n| change_ONWC | 22.629900 |\\n| change_operating_long_term_deposits | 0.052200 |\\n| change_goodwill | 0.000000 |\\n| change_IC | 33.480500 |\\n| change_NOPAT | 4.429950 |\\n| one_year_incremental_proxy | 13.23% |\\n| ROIC_average_IC | 17.90% |\\n| ROCE_average_IC | 23.86% |\\n| commissioned_asset_ROIC_excluding_CWIP | 19.40% |\\n\\nThe one-year capital bridge reconciles exactly at displayed primary precision: net cashcapex10.7984 +ONWC increase22.6299 +operating long-term deposit increase0.0522 =additionalIC33.4805. Both asset and funding approaches retain their0.0001 source-rounding difference. All CWIP is in historical IC. The commissioned-only return is a supplementary view excluding CWIP, not the default capital denominator. Finance-method IC excludes nonoperating loans/deposits, income-tax assets and investment assets, with excluded capital-creditor and tax funding added back consistently; full components are in the canonical table.\\n\\nI: The one-year incremental NOPAT/IC proxy is near the lower-middle of a12%–18% analyst cost-of-capital range, not proof that new projects earn attractive IRRs. It combines utilization, pricing, margin and capital timing; the CBG investment has not matured. Three-year incremental ROIC is NM because acquired capital, restatement and noncash classifications cannot be consistently attributed; five-year is ND because full comparable opening capital is missing. Missing project/cohort economics are not assigned a neutral CAP5. The net operating capital increase also differs from cashflow WC movement because cashflow includes FD movements, capital creditors and tax-related classifications; the two definitions are not interchangeable.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n| Metric | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| market_cap | 146.0858 |\\n| reported_parent_PE | 16.1202 |\\n| normalized_parent_PAT_excluding_all_other_income_at25pct | 8.3775 |\\n| book_price_equity | 2.9294 |\\n| normalized_gross_debt | 28.3579 |\\n| net_debt_available_cash_only | 26.3019 |\\n| net_debt_EBITDA | 1.7470 |\\n| external_sales_elimination | 6.1726 |\\n| normalized_parent_PE | 17.4378 |\\n\\nThe normalized parent-PAT sensitivity removes all other income at25% tax and allocates it using the FY26 minority/PAT mix. It is a deliberately broad analytical normalization, not a segmented tax/NCI reconciliation. FY25 other income includes0.90 subsidy; FY26 includes0.15 IPO subsidy and0.7606 interest. There is no assumption of a recurring subsidy annuity. A complete market enterprise value is NS because the market value of minority claims and availability of nonoperating assets are not verified. Parent market capitalization plus all group debt divided by all group EBITDA would mismatch claims; it is not presented as a clean EV/EBITDA multiple.\\n\\n### Cash-linked paths, not unrestricted extrapolations\\n\\n| Path | Status | First breach | Bank drawn | New shares | Terminal ₹/share | Ordinary IRR | Conditional IRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear_no_funding | BREACH | pre_entry month1 / gap₹6.6263cr | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bear_rescue | BREACH | pre_entry month2 / gap₹1.1913cr | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| base_no_funding | BREACH | pre_entry month1 / gap₹5.4613cr | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| base_rescue | BREACH | pre_entry month7 / gap₹0.4667cr | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bull_no_funding | BREACH | pre_entry month1 / gap₹5.8227cr | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bull_rescue | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | None in assumed path | 5.00 | 413218 | 286.84 | NS | 6.67% |\\n\\nA: The model begins31March2026 and uses seven equal elapsed-time buckets spanning202days to19October, then60 monthly holding buckets to19October2031. The pre-entry bridge is a **diagnostic interpolation, not actual April–October results**. Startingcash2.056 excludes the IPO reimbursement balance but assumes group pooling and physical cash availability; neither is independently established. Inventory/receivables/operating advances evolve with sales. Cash costs, taxes, interest, principal, capex and positive WC absorption are tested against partial receipts before remaining receipts arrive. Minimumcash0.50 and front-receipt timing are assumptions;30%/70%/100% sensitivities show timing dependence. A breached path stops immediately, including before entry; no ordinary return survives that stop.\\n\\nA: With no new funding, positive existing revolvers run off over twelve months as a conservative renewal-unavailable stress, **not a sourced lender recall schedule**. Optional rescue instead assumes the entire existing revolver renews at12%, an incremental bank cap5crore at12% with1% fee, and equity only after entry, at70% of the observed price with3% fee. The bank amortizes over36payments beginning modelmonth13. Equity never exceeds9,66,500 currently authorized headroom and is rounded upward to whole shares; extra legal issuance capacity, approvals and buyer demand are not assumed. No pre-entry equity is permitted because the observed share count must remain intact. A10crore bank-cap sensitivity is an additional hypothetical financing case, not a committed line. Financing fees, interest, debt repayment, dilution and NCI all affect returns.\\n\\n### Annual cash model\\n\\n| Path/year | Revenue | EBITDA | Tax | Interest | Capex | ΔWC | Principal | New loan | Equity gross | Fees | End cash | End debt | End shares |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bull_rescue/0 | 79.542 | 10.340 | 1.905 | 2.006 | 5.272 | -6.957 | 0.601 | 4.085 | 0.000 | 0.041 | 9.773 | 31.843 | 7033500 |\\n| bull_rescue/1 | 160.000 | 20.800 | 3.940 | 3.599 | 4.920 | 0.944 | 1.880 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 15.290 | 29.963 | 7033500 |\\n| bull_rescue/2 | 200.000 | 27.000 | 5.460 | 3.360 | 6.420 | 11.470 | 2.447 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 13.132 | 27.516 | 7033500 |\\n| bull_rescue/3 | 250.000 | 35.000 | 7.390 | 3.191 | 8.388 | 15.492 | 2.727 | 0.915 | 3.270 | 0.107 | 15.023 | 25.704 | 7258428 |\\n| bull_rescue/4 | 300.000 | 42.000 | 9.097 | 2.914 | 8.838 | 15.943 | 2.288 | 0.000 | 2.738 | 0.082 | 20.600 | 23.416 | 7446718 |\\n| bull_rescue/5 | 350.000 | 49.000 | 10.763 | 2.796 | 9.288 | 15.994 | 0.123 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 30.636 | 23.293 | 7446718 |\\n| base_bank10/0 | 73.217 | 8.786 | 1.533 | 1.997 | 2.815 | -0.563 | 0.601 | 5.471 | 0.000 | 0.055 | 6.034 | 33.229 | 7033500 |\\n| base_bank10/1 | 140.000 | 16.800 | 2.940 | 3.782 | 3.680 | 2.015 | 2.196 | 0.240 | 0.000 | 0.002 | 8.460 | 31.273 | 7033500 |\\n| base_bank10/2 | 160.000 | 19.200 | 3.527 | 3.650 | 3.920 | 7.684 | 3.494 | 2.231 | 0.000 | 0.022 | 7.592 | 30.009 | 7033500 |\\n| base_bank10/3 | 180.000 | 21.600 | 4.114 | 3.524 | 4.160 | 8.320 | 4.345 | 2.058 | 2.009 | 0.081 | 8.715 | 27.722 | 7171683 |\\n| base_bank10/4 | 200.000 | 24.000 | 4.785 | 3.059 | 4.400 | 8.391 | 4.306 | 0.000 | 2.867 | 0.086 | 10.556 | 23.416 | 7368910 |\\n| base_bank10/5 | 220.000 | 26.400 | 5.406 | 2.796 | 4.640 | 8.399 | 0.123 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 15.592 | 23.293 | 7368910 |\\n\\nYear0 is the202-day pre-entry bridge, not a fiscal year. Each monthly operating item is counted once despite two cash-event rows. Old-tax, capital-creditor and acquisition payments are separately included in the canonical annual identity. All shareholder wealth in this no-dividend model is exit-dependent; this is an explicit return model, not an assertion that terminal wealth is cash today.\\n\\n### Earned terminal economics\\n\\n| Path | Year6 revenue | FCFF | Cash reserve | EV | Earned EV/EBITDA | Debt | NCI fraction | Per share | 12% entry | 15% entry | 18% entry |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bull_rescue | 364.00 | 27.97 | 13.63 | 254.23 | 4.99 | 23.29 | 13.85% | 286.84 | 162.76 | 142.61 | 125.38 |\\n| base_bank10 | 228.80 | 13.24 | 9.16 | 120.39 | 4.38 | 23.29 | 13.85% | 121.03 | 68.68 | 60.17 | 52.90 |\\n\\nA: Terminal FCFF is next-year operating NOPAT plus depreciation less maintenance, incremental fixed assets, incremental ONWC and growth of the required operating-cash reserve. Enterprise value isFCFF/(WACC−growth), using15%/4% centrally and explicit12%–18% WACC and2%–6% growth sensitivities. The resulting EV/EBITDA is **earned from cash economics**, never a chosen exit multiple. Productive assets and incremental capacity remain funded in the explicit period. Year6 is rerun monthly with no fresh rescue; the full peak cash reserve is retained before any excess cash is added to equity, preventing distributed cash from funding the same working cycle. Terminal debt is deducted once. Stranded CWIP and unverified nonoperating recoveries are zero centrally.\\n\\nA: NCI13.8491% is FY26 minority PAT/group PAT, not the legal24.79% subsidiary ownership percentage; it proxies the economic share of group residual operating equity and is tested at0% and24.79%. Segment cash/debt allocation would be preferable. Terminal equity values are conditional diagnostics, not distributable cash today. Hurdle-entry prices at12%,15%,18% are terminal per-share values discounted five years while keeping the actual observed-price financing terms fixed. They are **not executable buy zones**: repricing an equity issue or changing actual bank terms requires rebuilding the model.\\n\\n| Conditional sensitivity | Status | Bank gross | New shares | Terminal ₹/share | Conditional IRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| base_bank10 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 121.03 | -10.24% |\\n| base_fd_release | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 3.47 | 0 | 127.39 | -9.31% |\\n| base_front30 | BREACH | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| base_front70 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 5.00 | 100011 | 128.38 | -9.17% |\\n| base_front100 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 0.00 | 0 | 137.68 | -7.89% |\\n| base_margin_wc_stress | BREACH | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| base_more_project_capex | BREACH | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| base_bank10_issue50 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 469573 | 118.87 | -10.56% |\\n| base_bank10_issue90 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 260874 | 122.27 | -10.06% |\\n| base_bank10_wacc12 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 173.81 | -3.50% |\\n| base_bank10_wacc18 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 90.87 | -15.24% |\\n| base_bank10_g02 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 119.69 | -10.44% |\\n| base_bank10_g06 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 122.97 | -9.95% |\\n| base_bank10_nci0 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 140.49 | -7.52% |\\n| base_bank10_nci2479 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 105.66 | -12.64% |\\n| bull_capital_intensity20 | BREACH | 5.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| bull_margin_minus2 | BREACH | 5.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| base_bank10_recovery2 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 123.75 | -9.84% |\\n| joint_margin-.02_wacc.12 | BREACH | 10.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| joint_margin-.02_wacc.15 | BREACH | 10.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| joint_margin-.02_wacc.18 | BREACH | 10.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| joint_margin0_wacc.12 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 173.81 | -3.50% |\\n| joint_margin0_wacc.15 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 121.03 | -10.24% |\\n| joint_margin0_wacc.18 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 90.87 | -15.24% |\\n| joint_margin.02_wacc.12 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 4.40 | 0 | 248.27 | 3.63% |\\n| joint_margin.02_wacc.15 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 4.40 | 0 | 178.59 | -2.97% |\\n| joint_margin.02_wacc.18 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 4.40 | 0 | 138.78 | -7.75% |\\n| bull_lower_terminal_multiple | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 5.00 | 413218 | 213.31 | 0.53% |\\n\\nThe source-safe canonical contains every scenario input, event row and terminal calculation, not just these summaries. A path showing a terminal value but failing its terminal funding test still has no supported IRR. CBG capex without earnings explicitly worsens funding. Deposit release and recoveries are assumptions tested separately; the same deposit is never both released and added again at exit.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations\\n\\n| Required equity return | First tested sustained base margin | Condition |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 12.00% | 22.00% | Discrete margin grid, not interpolated precision; requires optional10crore bank funding and rollover, existing capacity and capital bridge. |\\n| 15.00% | 24.00% | Discrete margin grid, not interpolated precision; requires optional10crore bank funding and rollover, existing capacity and capital bridge. |\\n| 18.00% | 26.00% | Discrete margin grid, not interpolated precision; requires optional10crore bank funding and rollover, existing capacity and capital bridge. |\\n\\nReverse tests hold the base sales path and capital/WC framework while changing sustained margin. The reported threshold is the first successful discrete2percentage-point grid value, not false interpolated precision. All tested rows are retained. Meeting a mathematical hurdle still depends on the hypothetical10crore bank envelope and renewal, not on price alone. Higher margin requirements must be judged against audited clean operating margins, tender competition and raw-material pass-through uncertainty. Accordingly P and R remain NS, rather than scoring a conditional financing assumption as a supported investment case.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nI: The strongest bear thesis is not that India stops using biofuels. It is that Rajputana sells more liters without generating enough cash: feedstock rises before tender realizations reset, finished goods/advances rise, delayed acceptance or input-tax realization locks cash, and lenders limit renewal just as capex and tax liabilities mature. Group cash may not be freely upstreamable; minority and creditor claims rank ahead of an optimistic parent-equity narrative. Optional equity may be unavailable, discounted further or constrained by authorized capital. CBG land and approvals can consume money before any saleable output.\\n\\n| Recovery stress | Haircut assets | Claims | Costs | Parent value | ₹/share | Loss vs quote |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| severe | 32.43 | 39.19 | 3.24 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 100.00% |\\n| moderate | 52.97 | 39.19 | 5.30 | 7.31 | 10.39 | 95.00% |\\n\\nA: Recovery stresses apply disclosed book assets with explicit realization haircuts, senior/operating claims,10% administration costs and a minority economic allocation. They are not legal creditor-priority opinions, appraisals, liquidation promises or post-breach IRRs. Unknown contingencies, tax leakage or inability to access physical cash could worsen recovery. Inventory and advances are not assumed worth face value simply because they appear in audited accounts.\\n\\nF/I: Contrary evidence matters: the company reports operating profit, growth, no default and an unmodified audit, and has existing manufacturing assets and customers. Those facts prevent describing modeled stress as an established insolvency. The thesis-break variable is verified cash survival and capital productivity, not a single missed sales quarter. An inability to renew facilities or actual material non-recoverability would be new adverse evidence; a reconciled liquidity bridge and repeat positive CFO would weaken the bear thesis.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\n| Years | Outcome | Required price CAGR | Sales at unchanged margin/PE/shares | Parent PAT | Above existing capacity proxy? |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 5 | 5× | 37.97% | 610.15 | 45.31 | True |\\n| 5 | 10× | 58.49% | 1220.29 | 90.62 | True |\\n| 10 | 5× | 17.46% | 610.15 | 45.31 | True |\\n| 10 | 10× | 25.89% | 1220.29 | 90.62 | True |\\n\\n| 5-year target | Terminal normalization | Tested margin range | Maximum diluted price multiple | Result |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 5× | earned_terminal | 8%–28% | 2.77× | NOT ATTAINED IN BOUNDED TEST DOMAIN |\\n| 10× | earned_terminal | 8%–28% | 2.77× | NOT ATTAINED IN BOUNDED TEST DOMAIN |\\n| 5× | lower_terminal_multiple_25pct | 8%–28% | 2.22× | NOT ATTAINED IN BOUNDED TEST DOMAIN |\\n| 10× | lower_terminal_multiple_25pct | 8%–28% | 2.22× | NOT ATTAINED IN BOUNDED TEST DOMAIN |\\n\\n**Funded outcome classification: Speculative / unsupported.** The full cash-linked base sales path140/160/180/200/220 is rerun over sustained margins8%–28% with all pre-entry and intraperiod cash tests, incremental productive capital, working capital, bounded bank financing, authorized whole-share dilution, minority claims and retained terminal cash. Every failed or successful trial is retained and mapped to its normalized full-path record. Neither5× nor10× is attained in the bounded five-year tested domain; the analysis does not extrapolate an unbounded margin root. These are conditional financing tests, not evidence that financing is available. A lower terminal multiple is explicitly tested by reducing the earned terminal enterprise value25%, while leaving operating cash, liquidity, debt and diluted shares unchanged. Failed paths remain failed and receive no return. The separate bull lower-terminal-multiple row in Section10 uses the same bridge. Accessible market share and expansion beyond stated capacity remain unverified, so no market-share gain is invented to make the target work.\\n\\nThe ten-year rows are mathematical decompositions only, not funded ten-year forecasts. These are mathematical requirements at unchanged earnings multiple, margins and shares, not targets. Even5× revenue on FY26 exceeds the408crore existing-capacity proxy;10× is further beyond it. Five-year multiples require very high per-share compounding, but a ten-year horizon does not remove feedstock, tender, incremental capital and cash constraints. Dilution, minority leakage, a lower exit valuation or a less favorable product mix raises required operating growth. CBG could theoretically expand the business, but approvals, capex, feedstock/offtake, unit economics and financing must be demonstrated before it enters an outcome case. No5×/10× claim is supported by the current evidence.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\nThis is a research checklist, not an automatically scheduled alert service.\\n\\n1. **Liquidity and refinancing:** obtain dated bank statements, utilization/renewal letters and cash restrictions, then reconcile every month fromMarch2026. Investigate any projected balance below the model's0.50crore operating minimum; actual overdue debt or unavailable renewals is a stronger thesis-break event than a modeled diagnostic.\\n2. **Cash conversion:** follow rolling two reporting periods of CFO excluding FD releases alongside reported CFO. Repeated negative conversion with positive earnings is an alert; two periods of genuine collections and declining financing dependence would support reconsideration. Do not credit deposit liquidation as customer collection.\\n3. **Inventory and supplier advances:** track finished goods28.4544, totalinventory35.7372 and doubtful advance0.4421. Require aging, subsequent sales, NRV/yield and supplier confirmations; rising aged/unsaleable balances without provision is a material impairment warning.\\n4. **Receivables:** reconcile disputed>3year0.6572 and older undisputed3.0617 with subsequent receipts and credit notes. Escalate deteriorating recoverability or unprovided disputes, not simply a higher nominal balance during verified volume growth.\\n5. **Product economics:** disclose liters, yields, feedstock mix/cost, tender realization, byproducts, trading and external intercompany eliminations. Compare the volume/price bridge over two comparable halves before assigning REV.\\n6. **Capital returns:** reconcile11.8497FY26 cash capex and8.3639CWIP to commissioned assets, budget, utilization and post-tax cash contribution. Require a mature cohort and organic/acquired bridge before CAP is scoreable.\\n7. **CBG gates:** land/title, establishment/environment approvals, signed EPC, feedstock cost/yield, offtake, commissioning and actual subsidy receipt must occur in sequence. Registration alone changes no earnings estimate. Further capex without committed finance triggers a fresh survival model.\\n8. **Governance and shareholder claims:** verify related-party repayment terms/recoveries, updated encumbrance, diluted shares, NCI distribution and audit explanations for historical restatements/EBITDA/EPS. An evidenced abusive transaction would change G1, not merely reduce a sentiment score.\\n9. **Execution:** compare allocation to dispatch and cash, plus declared project dates to actual commissioning. Preserve reasons for changed guidance and distinguish tenders won from profitable realized sales.\\n10. **Tradeability:** refresh60-/20-session traded values, live restrictions, circuit/quote depth and lot size before any proposed action; rerun the liquidity test for the user's actual position rather than assuming₹5lakh.\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Hold — unresolved; pending evidence, not ranking eligible.** This is a cash-constrained operating growth/special-situation research candidate, not an evidence-backed compounder. The framework requires BQS≥8 and GQS≥7, supported capital/cash evidence and clear relevant gates for that designation; this record has critical NS factors and unresolved G2/G3. No midpoint score, cheap-stock conclusion or buy/sell instruction is manufactured from the bounds. There is no supported ordinary base-case return and therefore no defensible action price at present.\\n\\nFor a new position, resolve current cash, renewals, dilution and execution feasibility before evaluating an entry price. For an existing holder, the issue is funding/cash quality and implementation, not merely a high valuation; a position-specific review would need size, liquidity, taxes and alternatives. The report does not execute or recommend a forced trade from a hypothetical breach. A lower price alone does not repair unavailable financing or unreliable capital attribution. Conversely, evidence of funded survival, repeat cash conversion and mature incremental returns could improve the assessment even without higher reported sales.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\n### Primary source ledger\\n\\n- **AR26** [Annual report FY2025–26](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/10th-Annual-Report-of-Rajputana-Biodiesel-Limited.pdf), dated 2026-08-22; Printed pp81–94,136–186; full audit, CARO, notes and financial statements read. SHA256 f0be2c16f377986ab1710734878331e492c2935f578b6938342bb6222695d45e.\\n- **AR25** [Annual report FY2024–25](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Annual-Report_2024-25.pdf), dated 2025-08-23; Printed pp63–166; full standalone/consolidated audit and comparative statements. SHA256 3abe29869e1c34002718c94ff445cd871eff93f2e91a55dcbe11b940670b0594.\\n- **IPO** [Final IPO prospectus](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Prospectus_Rajputana_Biodiesel_Limited_compressed.pdf), dated 2024-11-29; Printed pp29–47,73–79,96–109,135,140–155; F45–58 visually read, including restatement reconciliation. SHA256 d2e44508bc61ebc765338cc692b0dcc35fc2d850edc6cf8f8c267bc9b23bc0cf.\\n- **CALL25** [H1FY26 earnings call](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Transcript-of-conference-call_Rajputana_Dated-18th-November-2025.pdf), dated 2025-11-18; Printed pp3–6,8–12,17–20; management guidance, not achieved economics. SHA256 5f55df3f5409bcbadfa4bcbf489a7314e0205ffcdc94ac2fd098c4d0c27302b0.\\n- **PRES26** [FY26 presentation](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Rajputana_Investor-Presentation.pdf), dated 2026-05-27; Slides5–19; capacity, group structure, claims crosschecked to audited statements. SHA256 54c537ec63c3b5e7247a29a632bdd56e33a33712343c1cba37664f40cdc56da9.\\n- **CBG26** [CBG registration enlarged](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Rajputana_Enhancement-of-Registration-capacity-of-5-TPD-Compressed-Bio-Gas-CBG-Project-to-18-TPD-CBG-Project.pdf), dated 2026-10-06; All3pages; registration only, mandatory establishment approvals still to be sought. SHA256 f6b98a2603e34db1796509142cf6c95f4c1064fc0129d887f7db72dd286983bc.\\n- **ACQ26** [Arvant acquisition board approval](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Rajputana_OUTCOME-OF-BM_Dated-22nd-August-2026_Change-in-Auditors_Acquisition_AGM-1.pdf), dated 2026-08-22; All4pages; cash10000rupees,nil turnover,100% shares; auditor item is secretarial reappointment not statutory resignation. SHA256 3a2993214a8da5e5b428e0fa1ea389af269036e4f7f81b0894e85b01b7ad4442.\\n- **ACQDONE26** [Arvant acquisition completed](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Rajputana_Acquisition-of-Arvant-Bioenergy-Private-Limited_Completed.pdf), dated 2026-08-25; Onepage completion disclosure. SHA256 c706fc18e24f962848068e1759ccfab1fe1c54b6c2bb700f836e843cd7c5274c.\\n- **AGM26** [10th AGM transcript](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/10th-Annual-General-Meeting-Transcript.pdf), dated 2026-09-26; All10pages; no verified commissioned CBG financial outcome. SHA256 3c02b80a8a7a6c79a40fb4e03a53d034e86f73fe19a1f075612ce4b52fc74ddf.\\n- **LOI26** [Additional Jan2026 LOI](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Rajputana_LOI_Dated-5th-January-2026.pdf), dated 2026-01-05; Tender allocation is not recognized sale or cash collection. SHA256 ce022b1533fce1863bb3c8874eb0cbd7abb3fb51ad4ce37263f2918afd018921.\\n- **SHP26** [Shareholding31March2026](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Shareholding-Pattern-as-on-31st-March-2026.pdf), dated 2026-04; pp1–3 safe aggregate transcription; personal identifiers excluded. SHA256 9d88fb19a582dde1fc6c93b19fc8b783d3947d1821be4beaf0017dee9cd962cb.\\n\\nMarket evidence is the60 original NSE session archives through19October and the official security master; exact source URLs, archive/member hashes and issuer-only rows are in market_extract.json and security_extract.json. Public URLs identify source documents; original source files, personal identifiers, transport receipts and private credentials are not included. Operating peer source links are attached to their comparisons above, with date limitations disclosed.\\n\\n### Definitions and model reconstruction\\n\\nAll authored inputs, the standard-library Decimal engine, report template, builder and independent invariant checker are included in both compact and full packages. Run `python3 build_research.py` followed by `python3 verify_research.py` in the extracted folder. They require no network, PDF parsing, external package or source path. Canonical JSON retains unrounded numbers as decimal strings, all historical/calculation rows, factor terms and bounds, full scenario/reverse cash-event ledgers and source metadata. Generated outputs can be removed and regenerated; package manifests state the exact input/output allowlists and expected hashes. Validation checks cash and fixed-asset identities, whole-share bounds, no pre-entry equity, no continuation/IRR after breach, terminal reserve and score arithmetic. An archive recovery attestation is only valid for the exact downloaded archive hash, not for a prior local copy.\\n\\nKey analyst assumptions are five-year holding period;15% equity hurdle;12%–18% capital discount range;25% normalized tax;0.50cash floor; partial intra-period receipts; explicitly bounded optional finance; productive-capital intensity; maintenance rather than depreciation as cash capex; conservative nonoperating/CWIP value; and an economic NCI proxy tested over bounds. These are not company commitments. Valuation excludes unverified project subsidies/carbon receipts. No forecasts were used as historical factor evidence.\\n\\n### Unresolved evidence and falsification\\n\\n1. Actual October cash, bank utilization, facility renewals and restrictions, and cash fungibility across entities.\\n2. Comparable annual product volumes, realizations and raw-material yields, current customer/supplier concentration and tender dispatch versus allocation reconciliation.\\n3. Clean FY24 restated-to-comparative capital/cashflow reconciliation, long-run organic acquisition-capital bridge, and mature incremental project returns.\\n4. Recoverability and terms of related-party/other advances, aged/disputed receivables and doubtful supplier advances; inventory grade, saleability and NRV testing.\\n5. CBG land/title, complete permits, budget, EPC commitments, credible feedstock cost/yield, offtake and signed financing/subsidy eligibility and receipts.\\n6. Current promoter encumbrance, surveillance/circuit rules, executable quote depth and lot-constrained exit plan.\\n\\n### Update and quality log\\n\\nInitial v3.0 analysis with20October2026 information cutoff and19October market close. During review, parent-versus-group PAT, FY25 comparative EPS, overview EBITDA, FD-in-CFO classification, FY21 narrative endpoint, goodwill reclassification and source rounding were corrected explicitly. Cash modeling prohibits pre-entry equity, stops failed paths and reserves terminal operating liquidity before excess-cash valuation. This log records methodological corrections, not claimed changes in the company after cutoff. Research remains a hold until the listed evidence resolves; no pending evidence is treated as proven misconduct or as a neutral score.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\n**Hold — unresolved. A working-capital-intensive biodiesel growth situation, not an evidence-backed compounder.** Rajputana has expanded operating sales and parent-attributable earnings, with two biodiesel sites and repeat oil-marketing-company allocations. The decisive constraint is cash: every selected historical year has negative CFO, FY26 reported CFO benefited from a large FD release, and growth absorbed inventory, advances and new debt. Reported growth cannot be cleanly separated into organic volume, realization, trading and acquired scope. A headline earnings multiple does not compensate for an unverified current funding bridge. BQS, GQS and IAS therefore remain **NS**, not low-score substitutes; no investable ranking or ordinary expected return is assigned. Reconsider only after current liquidity/renewals, comparable product economics and incremental-capital evidence are established. Even hypothetical rescue returns do not make optional finance committed.\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\n| Item | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Issuer | Rajputana Biodiesel Limited / RAJPUTANA / INE0VHU01019 |\\n| Cutoff / quote | 2026-10-20 / 2026-10-19 |\\n| Price / shares / market cap | ₹207.70 / 7033500 / ₹146.0858 crore |\\n| Horizon / equity hurdle | 5 years / 15%; sensitivity12% and18% |\\n| Rubric / scope | v3.0 ordinary nonfinancial; SME forensic overlay; consolidated AS, parent-shareholder claims |\\n| Reference position | ₹5 lakh, not a suitability recommendation |\\n\\nF: Exact issuer identity is matched by ISIN in the official October19 NSE security master and daily tape, not by a similarly named biodiesel company. Incorporated10November2016; public-company conversion8July2024; SME listing3December2024. Current issued shares are70.335lakh, face value₹10, authorized80lakh. The financial record follows consolidated AS accounts and separately identifies parent claims and minority interests. Latest fully audited fiscal year is31March2026. Subsequent announcements only through the20October cutoff are used, despite research retrieval on23October. Later prices and postcutoff news are excluded.\\n\\n**Confidence:** High on FY25/FY26 audited primary P&L and current observed tape; Medium on balance-sheet classification, historical comparisons and capital allocation; Low on organic demand, mature incremental returns, actual October cash and refinancing. March2026 shareholding is verified but not relabeled as October. A qualified research hold is not an allegation of fraud or an assertion of current default.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Finding / resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 | Conditional | No substantiated severe integrity failure in audits reviewed; demand RPT recoverability and cash/FD availability require clarification. Obtain repayment schedules, confirmations, recoverability and board approvals for material related-party loans. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Latest audit is unmodified, but historical restatement/AR comparative differences and overview EBITDA versus P&L are material to a long-run incremental-return claim; no unfavorable allegation is inferred. Reconcile IPO restated FY24 capital/cashflow to later comparative, obtain consistent parent-attributable EPS and organic volume/price bridge. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | No proven default. Actual October cash, restrictions, headroom and renewals are not established; diagnostic pre-entry stress breaches without optional finance. Current bank statements, facility utilization/renewals, monthly collections, subsidiary fungibility and fully funded capex commitments. |\\n| G4 | Conditional | Observed trading and current lot established; order-book spread, circuits, current surveillance and execution feasibility remain unverified. Exchange restriction checks and executable quote/depth at time of proposed trade; do not treat master market-maker field as bid-ask spread. |\\n\\nF: March31 shareholder filing records six promoters holding46,26,000 shares(65.77%), public24,07,500(34.23%),808 holders and14,06,700 locked shares(20%). It declares no promoter pledge/other encumbrance, convertibles, warrants or outstanding ESOP as of that date. No September2026 primary filing was established. Shareholder identifiers are deliberately excluded from the data package. [SHP26 pp1–3]\\n\\n### Implementation and liquidity\\n\\n| Sessions | Mean ₹ | Median ₹ | Absent/zero | Days at2% | Days at5% | Days at10% |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 20 | 1425105.00 | 603062.50 | 4 | 41.455 | 16.582 | 8.291 |\\n| 60 | 1752649.17 | 670550.00 | 6 | 37.283 | 14.913 | 7.457 |\\n\\nF/A: The sample is60 exchange sessions through19October, not60 issuer trading observations. An absent issuer row is assigned zero capacity for the conservative liquidity calculation, not described as an observed zero-price bar. One500-share lot costs₹1,03,850. Four lots cost₹4,15,400; five exceed the₹5lakh reference. The60-session median-based starting L is3; lot indivisibility, absent sessions and unavailable spread/depth reduce it explicitly to2. At5% participation the median-day rupee allowance is smaller than one lot, so the fractional-day formula is not an executable sale schedule. No actual bid-ask spread is inferred from the security-master market-maker field. Verify live surveillance, circuit limits, quotes and lot rules before implementation. The reference amount is not a recommended allocation.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\n| Composite | Known contribution | Coverage | Raw missing-data bound | Cap | Final |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | 2.940 | 76.00% | 3.180–5.340 | None | NS |\\n| GQS | 1.950 | 45.00% | 2.500–7.450 | 5 | NS |\\n| IAS | NS | 30.00% | 1.845–6.435 | None | NS |\\n\\nAll weights remain original. Missing factors are not assigned5, not omitted from denominators and not renormalized. Bounds are mathematical missing-data bounds, not confidence intervals. GQS is capped at5 because CASH2 reflects persistent poor conversion; the final result remains NS. IAS uses nested BQS bounds, not a loose1–10 quality substitution. The literal IAS missing-factor bound is1.30–7.60 with Q/R/P NS; the table shows tighter nested propagation of the BQS evidence. Formal IAS coverage counts its original scoreable components; underlying weighted evidence coverage is also retained in canonical data. G1/G3 do not receive numeric failure caps because an evidenced severe integrity/funding failure has not been established. G2/G3 hold status independently prevents a favorable investment conclusion.\\n\\nBQS\\n| Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 0.20 | 5 | 1.000 |\\n| MOAT | 0.20 | 3 | 0.600 |\\n| REV | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| DUR | 0.10 | 5 | 0.500 |\\n| CAP | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| CASH | 0.10 | 2 | 0.200 |\\n| BAL | 0.08 | 3 | 0.240 |\\n| EXEC | 0.08 | 5 | 0.400 |\\n\\nGQS\\n| Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| REV | 0.40 | NS | NS |\\n| DUR | 0.20 | 5 | 1.000 |\\n| EARN | 0.15 | 5 | 0.750 |\\n| CAP | 0.15 | NS | NS |\\n| CASH | 0.10 | 2 | 0.200 |\\n\\nIAS\\n| Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS_final | .25 | NS | NS |\\n| R | .35 | NS | NS |\\n| D | .20 | 2 | 0.400 |\\n| P | .10 | NS | NS |\\n| L | .10 | 2 | 0.200 |\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence / contrary |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 5 | Medium | AR26 auditors report no identified fraud, defaults or statutory auditor resignation and an unmodified opinion. Related-party advances of 1.0378 crore to Deepprabha remain, and demand loans lack contractual repayment schedules; IPO proceeds fully deployed, including 12.20 crore working capital. Contrary: No substantiated diversion or minority abuse was established; related-party commercial dealings and short-duration director funding are disclosed. Demand-loan ambiguity warrants evidence, not a fraud allegation. [AR26 pp136–143,170–184] |\\n| MOAT | 3 | Medium | IPO risk discussion explicitly describes low entry barriers; customers are competitive OMC tenders rather than protected distribution. Spot feedstock purchases have no long-term supplier contracts, while top-ten customers accounted for 85.98% in FY24 and 100% in the July2024 stub. Contrary: Multi-feedstock process capability, BIS-qualified supply and repeat OMC allocations are useful operating capabilities, but durable price power or structurally superior cash returns is not demonstrated. [IPO pp29–47,96–109; CALL25] |\\n| REV | NS | Low for underlying demand; High for latest reported revenue | Reported FY23–26 revenue grows from 23.4085 to122.0292 crore, but March2024 acquisition makes the window non-constant scope. FY25–26 revenue grows81.29%, but audited annual volume/ASP and external intercompany elimination bridge do not establish organic demand separately from feedstock/pricing and trading. Contrary: Physical IPO production and FY26 parent/subsidiary sales confirm material scaling; neither a large reported CAGR nor capacity arithmetic supplies the missing comparable organic endpoint. [IPO F50–58 and p153; AR25; AR26 pp144–146; PRES26] |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | Existing group biodiesel capacity is stated as170KLPD across Phulera and Meerut, with repeat OMC allocations. An October6 CBG filing increases project registration to18TPD but still requires establishment approvals; no commissioned CBG earnings or binding subsidy proceeds are verified. Contrary: Renewable-fuel policy and byproducts provide plausible demand access; feedstock availability, tender price, dispatch and collection determine accessible economics, not national blending targets. [PRES26; CBG26; LOI26; CALL25] |\\n| CAP | NS | Low for incremental cohort economics | FY26 cash capex11.8497 crore and operating working-capital absorption are substantial, with8.3639 crore CWIP still uncommissioned. The three-year window spans acquisition, restatement and the1.0276 crore noncash goodwill/securities-premium reclassification; a clean organic/acquired invested-capital and mature-cohort bridge is unavailable. Contrary: FY26 normalized operating profit improves and a one-year accounting incremental proxy can be calculated. It does not establish returns on new CBG/land or distinguish improved utilization of old plants from new capital productivity. [AR26 pp144–166; AR25 goodwill rollforward; IPO F56–58] |\\n| CASH | 2 | High | CFO is negative for every FY22–26 year in the selected primary series; profitable FY24–26 CFO totals negative22.9126 crore. FY26 CFO negative2.8470 includes4.6488 crore FD release; removing that financial-asset release gives analytical CFO negative7.4958, while cash capex is11.8497. Contrary: FY26 receivables grow much less than revenue and FY25 CFO deterioration partly reflects deposits, but repeated inventory/advances absorption and aged balances prevent treating the issue as one transitory build. [AR26 pp146,160–166; AR25; IPO F52] |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | Normalized gross debt28.3579 crore includes23.0980 crore positive working-capital facilities; reported cash2.0582 includes1.3982 physical cash. No undrawn facilities are disclosed, while capital creditors1.9551, tax provision1.8855 and term maturities1.0854 require cash; all modeled no-new-funding branches breach in the diagnostic pre-entry bridge. Contrary: Auditors report no default, term debt amortization is modest and EBITDA covers recorded interest. The stress is not proof of actual default or lender withdrawal; actual October cash and renewals remain unresolved. [AR26 pp144–146,150–173; model_inputs.json] |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | Group manufacturing revenue has scaled after the Nirvaanraj acquisition, and both operating biodiesel sites are identified at170KLPD combined. The Nov2025 CBG/pellet diversification timetable is not demonstrated as delivered by cutoff; October2026 document remains a registration/approval-stage announcement. Contrary: Acquisition integration and new OMC awards are tangible progress, but announcements, allocated tenders and carbon-credit issuance do not establish commissioned project cash returns. [CALL25; PRES26; CBG26; AR26] |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium | FY26 parent-attributable PAT9.0623 exceeds FY25 restated comparative5.3663; clean operating EBITDA15.0556 excludes1.0598 other income. FY26 group PAT10.5191 includes1.4568 attributable to minority holders; using the group headline or FY25 old pre-NCI EPS would overstate shareholder earnings. Contrary: Operating growth is real, not wholly subsidy-driven; FY25 subsidy0.90 crore, FY26 subsidy0.15 and inconsistent overview EBITDA require normalization. CASH is scored separately, not double-counted here. [AR26 pp144–146,162–166; AR25] |\\n| R | NS | Low | Every unfinanced path stops at its first pre-entry cash breach and generates no ordinary IRR. Conditional rescue returns rely on uncommitted rollover, incremental loan and discounted equity; those cannot be substituted for a supported base-case investment return. Contrary: The bounded diagnostic can generate conditional prices and returns; numerical availability does not establish financing or current cash. [model_inputs.json; model_engine.py] |\\n| D | 2 | Medium | A linked feedstock-margin and working-capital stress requires incremental capital despite reported accounting profit. The bounded rescue bear branch still breaches before entry; the shareholder recovery diagnostic is severely exposed to debt and inventory/advance haircuts, rather than being assigned an ordinary post-breach IRR. Contrary: There is operating plant value and profitable demand, with no reported default. The low score represents a supported loss path, not a forecast of inevitable failure. [AR26; model_inputs.json; model_engine.py] |\\n| P | NS | Low | Reverse operating requirements are computed at the current price, but are conditional on cash availability and financing that are not established. CBG, carbon credits and future subsidies are excluded rather than used to justify today's valuation. Contrary: Reported parent earnings imply a moderate headline PE. A low headline multiple alone cannot establish undemanding enterprise expectations when cash funding is unresolved. [AR26; CBG26; model_engine.py] |\\n| L | 2 | Medium | Conservative60-session median gives14.913 days at5% participation for a5-lakh reference position, starting score3. Current official lot500 costs103850 rupees, exceeding5% of median daily value; six absent/no-trade sessions and unavailable bid-ask depth justify one-point downgrade. Contrary: The security did trade on October19 and mean turnover is higher than median; no suspension is inferred. Current surveillance/circuit/quote depth still needs live execution verification. [market_extract.json; security_extract.json] |\\n\\nJudgment sensitivity: changing GOV or EXEC by one point moves BQS bounds by0.20 or0.08 respectively, and IAS bounds by0.05 or0.02 through its25% BQS weight; GQS is unchanged. These small judgment effects do not resolve REV/CAP/R/P missingness or the gates. Conventional half-up rounding is used only for display; Decimal arithmetic and unrounded classification inputs are retained.\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\nF: The core activity converts non-edible oils, used cooking oil and other suitable feedstocks into biodiesel, with glycerine and fatty-acid byproducts. Parent Phulera capacity is stated at90KLPD and subsidiary Meerut at80KLPD. Nirvaanraj Energy was acquired28March2024 and is75.21% owned. FY26 parent sales₹59.0950 plus subsidiary sales₹69.1068 exceed group external sales₹122.0292 by₹6.1726 of eliminations. This prevents adding standalone and subsidiary turnover as if both were external. [AR26; PRES26]\\n\\nF/I: Customers include Indian Oil, Bharat Petroleum and Hindustan Petroleum through tender allocations. Tender quantity is not cash: pricing, call-offs, dispatch, product acceptance, credit and collection matter. No complete current order-to-dispatch-to-receipt rollforward was located, so order announcements are not capitalized as guaranteed backlog. Sales to a large state-owned customer reduce neither supplier pricing risk nor all collection/acceptance risk. The company also has byproduct and trading activity, making total revenue an imperfect physical production denominator. [IPO pp96–109; CALL25; LOI26]\\n\\nF: Agro LLP,99% owned, formed16March2026, has no FY26 turnover and is an agriculture/land-related early-stage investment. Arvant Bioenergy was acquired25August2026 for₹10,000 with nil reported turnover. Neither is assigned operating earnings or a premium simply because it may support future bioenergy expansion. [AR26 subsidiary schedule; ACQ26; ACQDONE26]\\n\\nA/I: At170KLPD ×300 assumed operating days ×1,000litres/KL ×₹80/litre, a rough biodiesel revenue capacity is₹408crore. This is a **unit arithmetic ceiling proxy**, not audited output, an addressable-market estimate or a forecast. The₹80 realization comes from a dated management discussion, not a guaranteed current contract. Product yield, density, grade mix, maintenance downtime and OMC orders can all lower realizable sales. No CBG or carbon-credit revenue is embedded in this ceiling or base valuation.\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\nF/I: Feedstock flexibility, conversion knowledge, acceptable specifications and repeat tender participation are useful but contestable capabilities. The IPO itself describes low entry barriers. Supplier concentration and spot purchasing without long-term supply contracts limit cost protection. There is no verified automatic pass-through or cost-plus contract that guarantees recovery of feedstock inflation; tender realizations can reset on a different timetable from raw-material purchases. A3percentage-point margin stress is therefore a cashflow scenario, not a macro slogan. [IPO pp29–47,96–109; CALL25]\\n\\nF: Top-ten customers were79.64% inFY22,89.72% inFY23,85.98% inFY24 and100% in the July2024 stub; the largest FY24 customer was26.16%, rising to39.25% in the stub. Top-ten supplier concentration rose to83.68% inFY24 and92.74% in the stub; the largest FY24 supplier was38.90%. These are **dated disclosures**, not claimed current concentration. Current FY26 customer/supplier breakdown and independent feedstock-cost advantage remain ND. [IPO concentration tables]\\n\\n| Operating comparator | Why relevant / limitation | Primary source |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Kotyark Industries | Closest listed biodiesel/glycerine tender/feedstock comparator; no unverified peer multiple used. Undated product description used only for business overlap. | [Source](https://www.kotyark.com/biodisel) |\\n| Universal Biofuels (Aemetis subsidiary) | India biodiesel/refined glycerin operator, competing OMC allocation model; February2025 restart approval illustrates plant/regulatory execution risk. Dated24February2025. US parent valuation not comparable. | [Source](https://aemetis.com/aemetis-india-plant-approved-by-government-to-restart-biodiesel-production/) |\\n| Emami Agrotech | Integrated edible-oil/biodiesel producer offers potentially stronger feedstock integration; unlisted, no clean comparable enterprise multiple. Business overlap only; current undated capacity and largest-producer marketing not imported into cutoff valuation. | [Source](https://www.emamigroup.com/group-of-companies/agrotech/bio-diesel-edible-oils-and-foods/) |\\n\\nI: These are operating comparisons, not a hand-picked valuation-multiple set. Kotyark is the closest listed biodiesel/glycerine analogue; Aemetis' US parent includes other businesses and financial risks; Emami is unlisted and integrated with edible oils. Their presence refutes an assumption of an uncontested market, but their promotional scale claims do not establish Rajputana's market share. No peer PE premium is imported into terminal value. No verified switching-cost, brand, network or patent mechanism supports a high moat score.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\n| Period | Start revenue | End revenue | Reported CAGR | Basis |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY23–FY26 | 23.4085 | 122.0292 | 73.39% | Non-constant scope; not scored |\\n| FY21–FY26 context only | 9.6900 | 122.0292 | 65.97% | FY21 MD&A context not restated, FY24 acquisition and price mix; not scored |\\n\\n| Year | Revenue addition | Reported YoY |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| FY2022 | NS | NS |\\n| FY2023 | 6.3364 | 37.12% |\\n| FY2024 | 30.0512 | 128.38% |\\n| FY2025 | 13.8534 | 25.91% |\\n| FY2026 | 54.7161 | 81.29% |\\n\\nF/I: FY26 growth is substantial, but the preferred five-year comparable organic window is unavailable. A FY21 revenue endpoint appears in IPO MD&A at₹9.6900crore with other income₹0.0811 and total₹9.7711; the early directors comparative gives total income₹9.7669, and nearby narrative year labels are inconsistent. It is retained as context rather than asserted to be a clean restated series. A three-year FY23–26 window is also acquisition-distorted because Nirvaanraj entered only near FY24 year-end. The numerical schedule would start at9 for a comparable CAGR above30%, but the basis required to apply that schedule is not established. REV is therefore NS, with no trajectory uplift or arbitrary small-base penalty. [IPO p153,F50–58; AR25; AR26]\\n\\nF: IPO parent production was1,908.61KL inFY22 and2,525.38KL inFY23. Later windows of4,355.33KL forApril2023–January2024 and4,246.01KL forFebruary–July2024 overlap fiscal boundaries and cannot be concatenated as annualFY24/FY25 volumes. Reported utilization figures refer to their own changing capacity windows. FY25 andFY26 audited annual volume/ASP/yield bridges were not established. Standalone FY25 sales fell from the FY24 presentation while consolidated sales rose; FY26 parent growth resumes, confirming that acquisition scope and allocation across entities matter. A claimed capacity increase is not evidence of organic volume growth without dispatch data.\\n\\nI: What is known is higher group revenue and operating profit. What is not known is how much came from external liters, traded material, byproduct mix, feedstock price inflation or stable-margin genuine demand. Future revenue assumptions in the model are explicitly analyst paths and never feed back into historical REV scoring.\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nF/I: Existing plants provide a plausible production runway, but incremental orders consume feedstock cash, finished inventory and tax credits before shareholder cash. Reported FY26 inventory₹35.7372 includes₹28.4544 finished goods and₹7.2828 raw material. GST ITC₹7.0476 is operating capital, not freely distributable cash. A national blending goal does not prove that Rajputana can source feedstock, win profitable tenders and finance inventory at the required scale. [AR26 notes16–20]\\n\\nA: The cash model uses annual sales paths bear110/105/110/115/120, base140/160/180/200/220 and bull160/200/250/300/350. Margins are7%→8%,12% flat and13%→14%, respectively. No policy subsidy, uncommissioned CBG, carbon-credit sale or Arvant earnings is used to balance cash. Working capital targets differ by case and move gradually, not via a free instant release. The bull requires materially better inventory and receivable efficiency than FY26; it is not a conservative extrapolation.\\n\\nA/I: Productive fixed assets roll forward by funded capex less depreciation. Each bucket must fund at least₹0.12 of incremental productive capital per₹1 of annual sales aboveFY26, in addition to maintenance. Existing8.3639 of CWIP remains committed capital historically but stranded for modeled earnings and terminal recovery. Sensitivity raises incremental capital intensity to0.20 and separately adds10crore unproductive project capex. This does not claim an engineering-validated plant budget: credible EPC, yields and commissioning economics remain missing.\\n\\nF/I: The6October2026 notice increases CBG project **registration** from5TPD to18TPD and states that mandatory establishment approvals will be sought. It is not commissioning, an executed EPC cost guarantee, a proven feedstock supply contract or a subsidy cash receipt. Management's earlier high CBG margin commentary is neither verified unit economics nor a reason to borrow a renewable-sector valuation multiple. CAP remains NS despite the calculable one-year accounting proxy below; GQS remains NS and capped at5 for poor cash realization.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\nF: The audited record discloses no reported fraud, default, willful-defaulter status or statutory auditor resignation forFY26, and the opinion is unmodified. The August announcement's auditor wording concerns a secretarial appointment, not evidence of a statutory auditor exit. Parent internal financial controls received an unmodified opinion; subsidiary applicability/exemptions mean that cannot be inflated into a fully tested group-wide controls claim. [AR26 standalone/consolidated audit,CARO,IFC; ACQ26]\\n\\nF/I: Related-party demand loans lack fixed repayment schedules, limiting the auditor's ability to comment on regular repayment. External Deepprabha advances stand at₹1.0378crore; disclosed subsidiary loans eliminate within the group and must not be counted again as an external asset. A disclosed related-party supplier purchase of₹6.9636crore including GST and temporary director borrowings deserve ordinary approval/terms/recoverability review, not automatic fraud language. Modest disclosed director remuneration does not neutralize capital-allocation or cash-access questions. [AR26 related-party and CARO notes]\\n\\nF: IPO proceeds were reported fully utilized:₹4.1873 subsidiary loan,₹12.20 working capital,₹5.8604 general corporate purposes and₹2.4523 issue expenses, totaling₹24.70crore. The fact that IPO working capital has been deployed does not imply a remaining funding buffer. The small₹0.0022 IPO account balance pending reimbursement is excluded from modeled available cash. No ordinary dividends are assumed. [AR26 note41]\\n\\n| Date | Claim | Delivery assessment | Source |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2025-11-18 | Improve utilization and commission diversification within following year; high CBG margin guidance | Biodiesel scaling supported; unit-volume utilization bridge absent. October6 CBG document only registration and approvals pending, no commissioning/cash proof. | CALL25;CBG26 |\\n| 2026-05-27 | 170KLPD biodiesel capacity and circular-business opportunity | Capacity claim retained as reported capacity, not production or normalized economic return. Carbon-credit cash receipts not verified. | PRES26;AR26 |\\n| 2026-08-22 | Acquire Arvant | CompletedAugust25 for10000rupees; zero-turnover vehicle not a cash-generating acquisition. | ACQ26;ACQDONE26 |\\n\\nI: Execution is mixed: operating plants and reported revenue have scaled, but diversification and cash realization have not been proven on the same timetable. No claim is made that registration-stage CBG is already overdue against a binding legal completion date. Monitoring should distinguish changed guidance, a credible revised funded plan and actual commissioned output.\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\n### Audited historical series and per-share perimeter\\n\\n| FY | Revenue | Op EBITDA | Group PAT | Parent PAT | CFO | Cash capex | FCF | Debt | Cash |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY2022 | 17.0721 | 1.2602 | 0.1997 | 0.1997 | -0.5277 | 0.3762 | -0.9039 | 10.9361 | 0.1675 |\\n| FY2023 | 23.4085 | 3.5086 | 1.6883 | 1.6883 | -1.1655 | 0.1273 | -1.2928 | 8.8712 | 0.7623 |\\n| FY2024 | 53.4597 | 7.5413 | 4.5617 | NS | -4.7375 | 0.9452 | -5.6827 | 19.1419 | 3.7185 |\\n| FY2025 | 67.3131 | 8.9096 | 5.9199 | 5.3663 | -15.3281 | 1.8606 | -17.1887 | 14.0268 | 1.5615 |\\n| FY2026 | 122.0292 | 15.0556 | 10.5191 | 9.0623 | -2.8470 | 11.8497 | -14.6967 | 28.1438 | 2.0582 |\\n\\n| FY | Inventory | Receivables | Payables | PPE | CWIP | Goodwill | FD | Owners equity | NCI |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY2022 | 2.3112 | 1.3126 | 0.6201 | 4.1932 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1337 | -1.2892 | 0.0000 |\\n| FY2023 | 5.2079 | 3.1037 | 1.3030 | 3.7637 | 0.0382 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 4.5499 | 0.0000 |\\n| FY2024 | 12.3960 | 12.7310 | 3.3073 | 5.5301 | 0.0000 | 1.1048 | NS | 14.2203 | 0.5826 |\\n| FY2025 | 18.8743 | 15.1997 | 2.1878 | 5.7609 | 0.7450 | 0.0772 | 8.7158 | 40.8373 | 1.1363 |\\n| FY2026 | 35.7372 | 15.6527 | 5.7236 | 8.9404 | 8.3639 | 0.0772 | 4.0670 | 49.8688 | 2.5950 |\\n\\nFY22/23 use IPO-restated historical statements; FY24 uses the later AR25 comparative, and FY25 uses the AR26 comparative. These are individually labeled source series, **not a seamless organic panel**. The alternative FY24 IPO figures are retained in canonical inputs: CFO−7.4507 versus−4.7375 in the later comparative, capex2.2327 versus0.9452, owner PAT4.5243 versus later group PAT4.5617, and materially different capital/working-capital presentation. They are not averaged. FY24 parent PAT is ND in the selected later comparative, so EPS/ROE are not invented. FY25 goodwill falls by1.0276 through an explicit pre-acquisition securities-premium reclassification, not impairment, sale proceeds or reduced investment. [IPO F50–58; AR25; AR26]\\n\\nF: FY26 group PAT10.5191 includes minority1.4568; shareholder PAT is9.0623. FY25 latest comparative shareholder PAT5.3663 and EPS9.33 replace an earlier pre-NCI headline5.9199 and oldEPS10.29. Current-share EPS differs from weighted-share reported EPS in the IPO year; the canonical table retains the denominator. The FY26 overview EBITDA15.8157 is not the clean P&L-derived15.0556: PBT13.4204 +finance1.6437 +DA1.0513 −other income1.0598. The0.7601 difference is exposed, not silently attributed to a particular adjustment without a reconciliation. FY25 prior-period/exceptional adjustments are separately retained; model normalization does not reward them as recurring operating income.\\n\\n| FY | EBITDA margin | CFO/PAT | EBIT/interest | AR days | Inventory days | AP days | CCC | Current-share EPS |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY2022 | 7.38% | -2.64 | 0.89 | NS | NS | NS | NS | 0.28 |\\n| FY2023 | 14.99% | -0.69 | 3.52 | 34.43 | 75.59 | 17.20 | 92.82 | 2.40 |\\n| FY2024 | 14.11% | -1.04 | 6.34 | 54.06 | 74.60 | 17.74 | 110.92 | NS |\\n| FY2025 | 13.24% | -2.59 | 4.48 | 75.73 | 105.87 | 18.70 | 162.89 | 7.63 |\\n| FY2026 | 12.34% | -0.27 | 8.52 | 46.14 | 98.85 | 12.68 | 132.31 | 12.88 |\\n\\nDays use average opening/closing balances. AR days use total sales as a credit-sales proxy. Inventory COGS includes raw material, traded purchases, direct expense and inventory change; payable days use raw-material-plus-traded purchases as an explicitly imperfect credit-purchases proxy because raw-material consumption is not exact purchases. Reported CFO is before financing cash interest: AR26 adds back finance cost and classifies cash interest in CFF. Therefore CFO-minus-capex is a reported pre-interest proxy, not debt-free FCFF or cash available to equity. FY25 cash finance1.7580 differs from P&L finance1.7915; FY26 cash finance is1.6437. After cash capex and financing cash interest, FY25 is−18.9467 andFY26−16.3404; after also removing the FY26 FD release, the latter is−20.9892. The forward engine separately deducts cash interest and does not reuse reported CFO as FCFF. Normalized NOPAT uses25% tax, with no unusable loss benefit; FY25 adds back the disclosed0.0709 prior-period and0.0020 exceptional charge, while the historical EBIT/EBITDA table retains the strict reported-PBT bridge. The incremental NOPAT comparison therefore uses normalized FY25 operating EBIT8.0977, not8.0248. Maintenance capex is not equated to accounting depreciation.\\n\\n### Earnings-to-cash and financing\\n\\n| Window | CFO | PAT | CFO/PAT | Capex | FCF |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 3 years | -22.9126 | 21.0007 | -1.09 | 14.6555 | -37.5681 |\\n| 5 years | -24.6058 | 22.8887 | -1.08 | 15.1590 | -39.7648 |\\n\\nF: FY26 PBT13.4204 plus1.9575 noncash/nonoperating adjustments produces pre-WC cash15.3779. Payables add3.5358 and other liabilities/provisions1.9009; receivables absorb0.4530, inventory16.8629, other current assets1.4787 and short-term advances2.9076. Tax paid1.9594 leaves CFO−2.8470. Cash capex11.8497 is an investing outflow, not the explanation for negative CFO. Cash after reported capex is−14.6967. CFI−9.1008 and CFF12.4444, against openingcash1.5615, imply closing2.0581; reported2.0582 differs by0.0001crore source rounding. The model does not fabricate an exact match. [AR26 p146]\\n\\nF/I: FD balances fell8.7158→4.0670, a4.6488 release folded into operating-current-asset movements. Removing that financing-asset release gives analytical CFO−7.4958, alongside the original−2.8470. This is a classification adjustment, not a claim the cash did not arrive. Do not both improve CFO with the release and count the original deposits as intact cash. FY25 FD accumulation is contrary evidence to treating all its CFO outflow as recurring cash cost; the comparable FY24 FD endpoint is not established, so a two-year deposit-adjusted conversion is ND rather than fabricated. Current FDs include0.142208 explicitly pledged to a bank guarantee; availability of the remainder requires evidence. Physical cash1.3982 of totalcash2.0582 and OD credit balances warrant access/confirmation checks. A negative0.2141 CC book balance is normalized out of gross debt, not treated as negative borrowing and simultaneously as free cash.\\n\\nF: Positive working-capital facilities total23.0980, term debt5.0648 and other debt0.1951, totaling normalizedgross28.3579. Reported debt28.1438 includes the negative CC. Current term maturity is1.0854; disclosed capex creditors1.9551 and tax provision1.8855 are separate cash demands. No undrawn facility was disclosed. Auditor non-default findings and EBIT/interest cover are positives, not proof of fresh lender headroom. No undisclosed lease debt is invented; operating rental and lease-equalization provisions remain within their accounting scope. [AR26 notes6–12,36]\\n\\nF/I: Disputed receivables over3years0.6572, undisputed1–2years2.8702 and2–3years0.1915 coexist with no impairment provision. Supplier advances include0.4421 explicitly doubtful. Such labels warrant customer/supplier confirmations, subsequent realization and NRV tests, not an automatic100% write-off. Gross-margin gains supported by finished goods rather than cash collections need inventory-grade/obsolescence evidence. No material contingent liability was disclosed, but a pledged guarantee FD means 'no disclosed contingent amount' is not the same as no guarantee exposure.\\n\\n### Incremental capital and return bridge\\n\\n| FY | ONWC | IC asset method | IC funding method | Rounding residual |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY2025 | 34.4636 | 41.9463 | 41.9462 | 0.0001 |\\n| FY2026 | 57.0935 | 75.4268 | 75.4267 | 0.0001 |\\n\\n| FY25→FY26 capital / return bridge | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| net_cash_capex | 10.798400 |\\n| change_ONWC | 22.629900 |\\n| change_operating_long_term_deposits | 0.052200 |\\n| change_goodwill | 0.000000 |\\n| change_IC | 33.480500 |\\n| change_NOPAT | 4.429950 |\\n| one_year_incremental_proxy | 13.23% |\\n| ROIC_average_IC | 17.90% |\\n| ROCE_average_IC | 23.86% |\\n| commissioned_asset_ROIC_excluding_CWIP | 19.40% |\\n\\nThe one-year capital bridge reconciles exactly at displayed primary precision: net cashcapex10.7984 +ONWC increase22.6299 +operating long-term deposit increase0.0522 =additionalIC33.4805. Both asset and funding approaches retain their0.0001 source-rounding difference. All CWIP is in historical IC. The commissioned-only return is a supplementary view excluding CWIP, not the default capital denominator. Finance-method IC excludes nonoperating loans/deposits, income-tax assets and investment assets, with excluded capital-creditor and tax funding added back consistently; full components are in the canonical table.\\n\\nI: The one-year incremental NOPAT/IC proxy is near the lower-middle of a12%–18% analyst cost-of-capital range, not proof that new projects earn attractive IRRs. It combines utilization, pricing, margin and capital timing; the CBG investment has not matured. Three-year incremental ROIC is NM because acquired capital, restatement and noncash classifications cannot be consistently attributed; five-year is ND because full comparable opening capital is missing. Missing project/cohort economics are not assigned a neutral CAP5. The net operating capital increase also differs from cashflow WC movement because cashflow includes FD movements, capital creditors and tax-related classifications; the two definitions are not interchangeable.\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\n| Metric | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| market_cap | 146.0858 |\\n| reported_parent_PE | 16.1202 |\\n| normalized_parent_PAT_excluding_all_other_income_at25pct | 8.3775 |\\n| book_price_equity | 2.9294 |\\n| normalized_gross_debt | 28.3579 |\\n| net_debt_available_cash_only | 26.3019 |\\n| net_debt_EBITDA | 1.7470 |\\n| external_sales_elimination | 6.1726 |\\n| normalized_parent_PE | 17.4378 |\\n\\nThe normalized parent-PAT sensitivity removes all other income at25% tax and allocates it using the FY26 minority/PAT mix. It is a deliberately broad analytical normalization, not a segmented tax/NCI reconciliation. FY25 other income includes0.90 subsidy; FY26 includes0.15 IPO subsidy and0.7606 interest. There is no assumption of a recurring subsidy annuity. A complete market enterprise value is NS because the market value of minority claims and availability of nonoperating assets are not verified. Parent market capitalization plus all group debt divided by all group EBITDA would mismatch claims; it is not presented as a clean EV/EBITDA multiple.\\n\\n### Cash-linked paths, not unrestricted extrapolations\\n\\n| Path | Status | First breach | Bank drawn | New shares | Terminal ₹/share | Ordinary IRR | Conditional IRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear_no_funding | BREACH | pre_entry month1 / gap₹6.6263cr | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bear_rescue | BREACH | pre_entry month2 / gap₹1.1913cr | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| base_no_funding | BREACH | pre_entry month1 / gap₹5.4613cr | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| base_rescue | BREACH | pre_entry month7 / gap₹0.4667cr | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bull_no_funding | BREACH | pre_entry month1 / gap₹5.8227cr | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bull_rescue | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | None in assumed path | 5.00 | 413218 | 286.84 | NS | 6.67% |\\n\\nA: The model begins31March2026 and uses seven equal elapsed-time buckets spanning202days to19October, then60 monthly holding buckets to19October2031. The pre-entry bridge is a **diagnostic interpolation, not actual April–October results**. Startingcash2.056 excludes the IPO reimbursement balance but assumes group pooling and physical cash availability; neither is independently established. Inventory/receivables/operating advances evolve with sales. Cash costs, taxes, interest, principal, capex and positive WC absorption are tested against partial receipts before remaining receipts arrive. Minimumcash0.50 and front-receipt timing are assumptions;30%/70%/100% sensitivities show timing dependence. A breached path stops immediately, including before entry; no ordinary return survives that stop.\\n\\nA: With no new funding, positive existing revolvers run off over twelve months as a conservative renewal-unavailable stress, **not a sourced lender recall schedule**. Optional rescue instead assumes the entire existing revolver renews at12%, an incremental bank cap5crore at12% with1% fee, and equity only after entry, at70% of the observed price with3% fee. The bank amortizes over36payments beginning modelmonth13. Equity never exceeds9,66,500 currently authorized headroom and is rounded upward to whole shares; extra legal issuance capacity, approvals and buyer demand are not assumed. No pre-entry equity is permitted because the observed share count must remain intact. A10crore bank-cap sensitivity is an additional hypothetical financing case, not a committed line. Financing fees, interest, debt repayment, dilution and NCI all affect returns.\\n\\n### Annual cash model\\n\\n| Path/year | Revenue | EBITDA | Tax | Interest | Capex | ΔWC | Principal | New loan | Equity gross | Fees | End cash | End debt | End shares |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bull_rescue/0 | 79.542 | 10.340 | 1.905 | 2.006 | 5.272 | -6.957 | 0.601 | 4.085 | 0.000 | 0.041 | 9.773 | 31.843 | 7033500 |\\n| bull_rescue/1 | 160.000 | 20.800 | 3.940 | 3.599 | 4.920 | 0.944 | 1.880 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 15.290 | 29.963 | 7033500 |\\n| bull_rescue/2 | 200.000 | 27.000 | 5.460 | 3.360 | 6.420 | 11.470 | 2.447 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 13.132 | 27.516 | 7033500 |\\n| bull_rescue/3 | 250.000 | 35.000 | 7.390 | 3.191 | 8.388 | 15.492 | 2.727 | 0.915 | 3.270 | 0.107 | 15.023 | 25.704 | 7258428 |\\n| bull_rescue/4 | 300.000 | 42.000 | 9.097 | 2.914 | 8.838 | 15.943 | 2.288 | 0.000 | 2.738 | 0.082 | 20.600 | 23.416 | 7446718 |\\n| bull_rescue/5 | 350.000 | 49.000 | 10.763 | 2.796 | 9.288 | 15.994 | 0.123 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 30.636 | 23.293 | 7446718 |\\n| base_bank10/0 | 73.217 | 8.786 | 1.533 | 1.997 | 2.815 | -0.563 | 0.601 | 5.471 | 0.000 | 0.055 | 6.034 | 33.229 | 7033500 |\\n| base_bank10/1 | 140.000 | 16.800 | 2.940 | 3.782 | 3.680 | 2.015 | 2.196 | 0.240 | 0.000 | 0.002 | 8.460 | 31.273 | 7033500 |\\n| base_bank10/2 | 160.000 | 19.200 | 3.527 | 3.650 | 3.920 | 7.684 | 3.494 | 2.231 | 0.000 | 0.022 | 7.592 | 30.009 | 7033500 |\\n| base_bank10/3 | 180.000 | 21.600 | 4.114 | 3.524 | 4.160 | 8.320 | 4.345 | 2.058 | 2.009 | 0.081 | 8.715 | 27.722 | 7171683 |\\n| base_bank10/4 | 200.000 | 24.000 | 4.785 | 3.059 | 4.400 | 8.391 | 4.306 | 0.000 | 2.867 | 0.086 | 10.556 | 23.416 | 7368910 |\\n| base_bank10/5 | 220.000 | 26.400 | 5.406 | 2.796 | 4.640 | 8.399 | 0.123 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 15.592 | 23.293 | 7368910 |\\n\\nYear0 is the202-day pre-entry bridge, not a fiscal year. Each monthly operating item is counted once despite two cash-event rows. Old-tax, capital-creditor and acquisition payments are separately included in the canonical annual identity. All shareholder wealth in this no-dividend model is exit-dependent; this is an explicit return model, not an assertion that terminal wealth is cash today.\\n\\n### Earned terminal economics\\n\\n| Path | Year6 revenue | FCFF | Cash reserve | EV | Earned EV/EBITDA | Debt | NCI fraction | Per share | 12% entry | 15% entry | 18% entry |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bull_rescue | 364.00 | 27.97 | 13.63 | 254.23 | 4.99 | 23.29 | 13.85% | 286.84 | 162.76 | 142.61 | 125.38 |\\n| base_bank10 | 228.80 | 13.24 | 9.16 | 120.39 | 4.38 | 23.29 | 13.85% | 121.03 | 68.68 | 60.17 | 52.90 |\\n\\nA: Terminal FCFF is next-year operating NOPAT plus depreciation less maintenance, incremental fixed assets, incremental ONWC and growth of the required operating-cash reserve. Enterprise value isFCFF/(WACC−growth), using15%/4% centrally and explicit12%–18% WACC and2%–6% growth sensitivities. The resulting EV/EBITDA is **earned from cash economics**, never a chosen exit multiple. Productive assets and incremental capacity remain funded in the explicit period. Year6 is rerun monthly with no fresh rescue; the full peak cash reserve is retained before any excess cash is added to equity, preventing distributed cash from funding the same working cycle. Terminal debt is deducted once. Stranded CWIP and unverified nonoperating recoveries are zero centrally.\\n\\nA: NCI13.8491% is FY26 minority PAT/group PAT, not the legal24.79% subsidiary ownership percentage; it proxies the economic share of group residual operating equity and is tested at0% and24.79%. Segment cash/debt allocation would be preferable. Terminal equity values are conditional diagnostics, not distributable cash today. Hurdle-entry prices at12%,15%,18% are terminal per-share values discounted five years while keeping the actual observed-price financing terms fixed. They are **not executable buy zones**: repricing an equity issue or changing actual bank terms requires rebuilding the model.\\n\\n| Conditional sensitivity | Status | Bank gross | New shares | Terminal ₹/share | Conditional IRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| base_bank10 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 121.03 | -10.24% |\\n| base_fd_release | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 3.47 | 0 | 127.39 | -9.31% |\\n| base_front30 | BREACH | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| base_front70 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 5.00 | 100011 | 128.38 | -9.17% |\\n| base_front100 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 0.00 | 0 | 137.68 | -7.89% |\\n| base_margin_wc_stress | BREACH | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| base_more_project_capex | BREACH | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| base_bank10_issue50 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 469573 | 118.87 | -10.56% |\\n| base_bank10_issue90 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 260874 | 122.27 | -10.06% |\\n| base_bank10_wacc12 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 173.81 | -3.50% |\\n| base_bank10_wacc18 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 90.87 | -15.24% |\\n| base_bank10_g02 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 119.69 | -10.44% |\\n| base_bank10_g06 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 122.97 | -9.95% |\\n| base_bank10_nci0 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 140.49 | -7.52% |\\n| base_bank10_nci2479 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 105.66 | -12.64% |\\n| bull_capital_intensity20 | BREACH | 5.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| bull_margin_minus2 | BREACH | 5.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| base_bank10_recovery2 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 123.75 | -9.84% |\\n| joint_margin-.02_wacc.12 | BREACH | 10.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| joint_margin-.02_wacc.15 | BREACH | 10.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| joint_margin-.02_wacc.18 | BREACH | 10.00 | 966500 | NS | NS |\\n| joint_margin0_wacc.12 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 173.81 | -3.50% |\\n| joint_margin0_wacc.15 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 121.03 | -10.24% |\\n| joint_margin0_wacc.18 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 10.00 | 335410 | 90.87 | -15.24% |\\n| joint_margin.02_wacc.12 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 4.40 | 0 | 248.27 | 3.63% |\\n| joint_margin.02_wacc.15 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 4.40 | 0 | 178.59 | -2.97% |\\n| joint_margin.02_wacc.18 | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 4.40 | 0 | 138.78 | -7.75% |\\n| bull_lower_terminal_multiple | SURVIVES_ASSUMED_PATH | 5.00 | 413218 | 213.31 | 0.53% |\\n\\nThe source-safe canonical contains every scenario input, event row and terminal calculation, not just these summaries. A path showing a terminal value but failing its terminal funding test still has no supported IRR. CBG capex without earnings explicitly worsens funding. Deposit release and recoveries are assumptions tested separately; the same deposit is never both released and added again at exit.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations\\n\\n| Required equity return | First tested sustained base margin | Condition |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 12.00% | 22.00% | Discrete margin grid, not interpolated precision; requires optional10crore bank funding and rollover, existing capacity and capital bridge. |\\n| 15.00% | 24.00% | Discrete margin grid, not interpolated precision; requires optional10crore bank funding and rollover, existing capacity and capital bridge. |\\n| 18.00% | 26.00% | Discrete margin grid, not interpolated precision; requires optional10crore bank funding and rollover, existing capacity and capital bridge. |\\n\\nReverse tests hold the base sales path and capital/WC framework while changing sustained margin. The reported threshold is the first successful discrete2percentage-point grid value, not false interpolated precision. All tested rows are retained. Meeting a mathematical hurdle still depends on the hypothetical10crore bank envelope and renewal, not on price alone. Higher margin requirements must be judged against audited clean operating margins, tender competition and raw-material pass-through uncertainty. Accordingly P and R remain NS, rather than scoring a conditional financing assumption as a supported investment case.\\n\\n\"","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\nI: The strongest bear thesis is not that India stops using biofuels. It is that Rajputana sells more liters without generating enough cash: feedstock rises before tender realizations reset, finished goods/advances rise, delayed acceptance or input-tax realization locks cash, and lenders limit renewal just as capex and tax liabilities mature. Group cash may not be freely upstreamable; minority and creditor claims rank ahead of an optimistic parent-equity narrative. Optional equity may be unavailable, discounted further or constrained by authorized capital. CBG land and approvals can consume money before any saleable output.\\n\\n| Recovery stress | Haircut assets | Claims | Costs | Parent value | ₹/share | Loss vs quote |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| severe | 32.43 | 39.19 | 3.24 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 100.00% |\\n| moderate | 52.97 | 39.19 | 5.30 | 7.31 | 10.39 | 95.00% |\\n\\nA: Recovery stresses apply disclosed book assets with explicit realization haircuts, senior/operating claims,10% administration costs and a minority economic allocation. They are not legal creditor-priority opinions, appraisals, liquidation promises or post-breach IRRs. Unknown contingencies, tax leakage or inability to access physical cash could worsen recovery. Inventory and advances are not assumed worth face value simply because they appear in audited accounts.\\n\\nF/I: Contrary evidence matters: the company reports operating profit, growth, no default and an unmodified audit, and has existing manufacturing assets and customers. Those facts prevent describing modeled stress as an established insolvency. The thesis-break variable is verified cash survival and capital productivity, not a single missed sales quarter. An inability to renew facilities or actual material non-recoverability would be new adverse evidence; a reconciled liquidity bridge and repeat positive CFO would weaken the bear thesis.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\n| Years | Outcome | Required price CAGR | Sales at unchanged margin/PE/shares | Parent PAT | Above existing capacity proxy? |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 5 | 5× | 37.97% | 610.15 | 45.31 | True |\\n| 5 | 10× | 58.49% | 1220.29 | 90.62 | True |\\n| 10 | 5× | 17.46% | 610.15 | 45.31 | True |\\n| 10 | 10× | 25.89% | 1220.29 | 90.62 | True |\\n\\n| 5-year target | Terminal normalization | Tested margin range | Maximum diluted price multiple | Result |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 5× | earned_terminal | 8%–28% | 2.77× | NOT ATTAINED IN BOUNDED TEST DOMAIN |\\n| 10× | earned_terminal | 8%–28% | 2.77× | NOT ATTAINED IN BOUNDED TEST DOMAIN |\\n| 5× | lower_terminal_multiple_25pct | 8%–28% | 2.22× | NOT ATTAINED IN BOUNDED TEST DOMAIN |\\n| 10× | lower_terminal_multiple_25pct | 8%–28% | 2.22× | NOT ATTAINED IN BOUNDED TEST DOMAIN |\\n\\n**Funded outcome classification: Speculative / unsupported.** The full cash-linked base sales path140/160/180/200/220 is rerun over sustained margins8%–28% with all pre-entry and intraperiod cash tests, incremental productive capital, working capital, bounded bank financing, authorized whole-share dilution, minority claims and retained terminal cash. Every failed or successful trial is retained and mapped to its normalized full-path record. Neither5× nor10× is attained in the bounded five-year tested domain; the analysis does not extrapolate an unbounded margin root. These are conditional financing tests, not evidence that financing is available. A lower terminal multiple is explicitly tested by reducing the earned terminal enterprise value25%, while leaving operating cash, liquidity, debt and diluted shares unchanged. Failed paths remain failed and receive no return. The separate bull lower-terminal-multiple row in Section10 uses the same bridge. Accessible market share and expansion beyond stated capacity remain unverified, so no market-share gain is invented to make the target work.\\n\\nThe ten-year rows are mathematical decompositions only, not funded ten-year forecasts. These are mathematical requirements at unchanged earnings multiple, margins and shares, not targets. Even5× revenue on FY26 exceeds the408crore existing-capacity proxy;10× is further beyond it. Five-year multiples require very high per-share compounding, but a ten-year horizon does not remove feedstock, tender, incremental capital and cash constraints. Dilution, minority leakage, a lower exit valuation or a less favorable product mix raises required operating growth. CBG could theoretically expand the business, but approvals, capex, feedstock/offtake, unit economics and financing must be demonstrated before it enters an outcome case. No5×/10× claim is supported by the current evidence.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\nThis is a research checklist, not an automatically scheduled alert service.\\n\\n1. **Liquidity and refinancing:** obtain dated bank statements, utilization/renewal letters and cash restrictions, then reconcile every month fromMarch2026. Investigate any projected balance below the model's0.50crore operating minimum; actual overdue debt or unavailable renewals is a stronger thesis-break event than a modeled diagnostic.\\n2. **Cash conversion:** follow rolling two reporting periods of CFO excluding FD releases alongside reported CFO. Repeated negative conversion with positive earnings is an alert; two periods of genuine collections and declining financing dependence would support reconsideration. Do not credit deposit liquidation as customer collection.\\n3. **Inventory and supplier advances:** track finished goods28.4544, totalinventory35.7372 and doubtful advance0.4421. Require aging, subsequent sales, NRV/yield and supplier confirmations; rising aged/unsaleable balances without provision is a material impairment warning.\\n4. **Receivables:** reconcile disputed>3year0.6572 and older undisputed3.0617 with subsequent receipts and credit notes. Escalate deteriorating recoverability or unprovided disputes, not simply a higher nominal balance during verified volume growth.\\n5. **Product economics:** disclose liters, yields, feedstock mix/cost, tender realization, byproducts, trading and external intercompany eliminations. Compare the volume/price bridge over two comparable halves before assigning REV.\\n6. **Capital returns:** reconcile11.8497FY26 cash capex and8.3639CWIP to commissioned assets, budget, utilization and post-tax cash contribution. Require a mature cohort and organic/acquired bridge before CAP is scoreable.\\n7. **CBG gates:** land/title, establishment/environment approvals, signed EPC, feedstock cost/yield, offtake, commissioning and actual subsidy receipt must occur in sequence. Registration alone changes no earnings estimate. Further capex without committed finance triggers a fresh survival model.\\n8. **Governance and shareholder claims:** verify related-party repayment terms/recoveries, updated encumbrance, diluted shares, NCI distribution and audit explanations for historical restatements/EBITDA/EPS. An evidenced abusive transaction would change G1, not merely reduce a sentiment score.\\n9. **Execution:** compare allocation to dispatch and cash, plus declared project dates to actual commissioning. Preserve reasons for changed guidance and distinguish tenders won from profitable realized sales.\\n10. **Tradeability:** refresh60-/20-session traded values, live restrictions, circuit/quote depth and lot size before any proposed action; rerun the liquidity test for the user's actual position rather than assuming₹5lakh.\\n\\n\"","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\n**Hold — unresolved; pending evidence, not ranking eligible.** This is a cash-constrained operating growth/special-situation research candidate, not an evidence-backed compounder. The framework requires BQS≥8 and GQS≥7, supported capital/cash evidence and clear relevant gates for that designation; this record has critical NS factors and unresolved G2/G3. No midpoint score, cheap-stock conclusion or buy/sell instruction is manufactured from the bounds. There is no supported ordinary base-case return and therefore no defensible action price at present.\\n\\nFor a new position, resolve current cash, renewals, dilution and execution feasibility before evaluating an entry price. For an existing holder, the issue is funding/cash quality and implementation, not merely a high valuation; a position-specific review would need size, liquidity, taxes and alternatives. The report does not execute or recommend a forced trade from a hypothetical breach. A lower price alone does not repair unavailable financing or unreliable capital attribution. Conversely, evidence of funded survival, repeat cash conversion and mature incremental returns could improve the assessment even without higher reported sales.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\n### Primary source ledger\\n\\n- **AR26** [Annual report FY2025–26](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/10th-Annual-Report-of-Rajputana-Biodiesel-Limited.pdf), dated 2026-08-22; Printed pp81–94,136–186; full audit, CARO, notes and financial statements read. SHA256 f0be2c16f377986ab1710734878331e492c2935f578b6938342bb6222695d45e.\\n- **AR25** [Annual report FY2024–25](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Annual-Report_2024-25.pdf), dated 2025-08-23; Printed pp63–166; full standalone/consolidated audit and comparative statements. SHA256 3abe29869e1c34002718c94ff445cd871eff93f2e91a55dcbe11b940670b0594.\\n- **IPO** [Final IPO prospectus](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Prospectus_Rajputana_Biodiesel_Limited_compressed.pdf), dated 2024-11-29; Printed pp29–47,73–79,96–109,135,140–155; F45–58 visually read, including restatement reconciliation. SHA256 d2e44508bc61ebc765338cc692b0dcc35fc2d850edc6cf8f8c267bc9b23bc0cf.\\n- **CALL25** [H1FY26 earnings call](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Transcript-of-conference-call_Rajputana_Dated-18th-November-2025.pdf), dated 2025-11-18; Printed pp3–6,8–12,17–20; management guidance, not achieved economics. SHA256 5f55df3f5409bcbadfa4bcbf489a7314e0205ffcdc94ac2fd098c4d0c27302b0.\\n- **PRES26** [FY26 presentation](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Rajputana_Investor-Presentation.pdf), dated 2026-05-27; Slides5–19; capacity, group structure, claims crosschecked to audited statements. SHA256 54c537ec63c3b5e7247a29a632bdd56e33a33712343c1cba37664f40cdc56da9.\\n- **CBG26** [CBG registration enlarged](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Rajputana_Enhancement-of-Registration-capacity-of-5-TPD-Compressed-Bio-Gas-CBG-Project-to-18-TPD-CBG-Project.pdf), dated 2026-10-06; All3pages; registration only, mandatory establishment approvals still to be sought. SHA256 f6b98a2603e34db1796509142cf6c95f4c1064fc0129d887f7db72dd286983bc.\\n- **ACQ26** [Arvant acquisition board approval](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Rajputana_OUTCOME-OF-BM_Dated-22nd-August-2026_Change-in-Auditors_Acquisition_AGM-1.pdf), dated 2026-08-22; All4pages; cash10000rupees,nil turnover,100% shares; auditor item is secretarial reappointment not statutory resignation. SHA256 3a2993214a8da5e5b428e0fa1ea389af269036e4f7f81b0894e85b01b7ad4442.\\n- **ACQDONE26** [Arvant acquisition completed](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Rajputana_Acquisition-of-Arvant-Bioenergy-Private-Limited_Completed.pdf), dated 2026-08-25; Onepage completion disclosure. SHA256 c706fc18e24f962848068e1759ccfab1fe1c54b6c2bb700f836e843cd7c5274c.\\n- **AGM26** [10th AGM transcript](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/10th-Annual-General-Meeting-Transcript.pdf), dated 2026-09-26; All10pages; no verified commissioned CBG financial outcome. SHA256 3c02b80a8a7a6c79a40fb4e03a53d034e86f73fe19a1f075612ce4b52fc74ddf.\\n- **LOI26** [Additional Jan2026 LOI](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Rajputana_LOI_Dated-5th-January-2026.pdf), dated 2026-01-05; Tender allocation is not recognized sale or cash collection. SHA256 ce022b1533fce1863bb3c8874eb0cbd7abb3fb51ad4ce37263f2918afd018921.\\n- **SHP26** [Shareholding31March2026](https://rajputanabiodiesel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Shareholding-Pattern-as-on-31st-March-2026.pdf), dated 2026-04; pp1–3 safe aggregate transcription; personal identifiers excluded. SHA256 9d88fb19a582dde1fc6c93b19fc8b783d3947d1821be4beaf0017dee9cd962cb.\\n\\nMarket evidence is the60 original NSE session archives through19October and the official security master; exact source URLs, archive/member hashes and issuer-only rows are in market_extract.json and security_extract.json. Public URLs identify source documents; original source files, personal identifiers, transport receipts and private credentials are not included. Operating peer source links are attached to their comparisons above, with date limitations disclosed.\\n\\n### Definitions and model reconstruction\\n\\nAll authored inputs, the standard-library Decimal engine, report template, builder and independent invariant checker are included in both compact and full packages. Run `python3 build_research.py` followed by `python3 verify_research.py` in the extracted folder. They require no network, PDF parsing, external package or source path. Canonical JSON retains unrounded numbers as decimal strings, all historical/calculation rows, factor terms and bounds, full scenario/reverse cash-event ledgers and source metadata. Generated outputs can be removed and regenerated; package manifests state the exact input/output allowlists and expected hashes. Validation checks cash and fixed-asset identities, whole-share bounds, no pre-entry equity, no continuation/IRR after breach, terminal reserve and score arithmetic. An archive recovery attestation is only valid for the exact downloaded archive hash, not for a prior local copy.\\n\\nKey analyst assumptions are five-year holding period;15% equity hurdle;12%–18% capital discount range;25% normalized tax;0.50cash floor; partial intra-period receipts; explicitly bounded optional finance; productive-capital intensity; maintenance rather than depreciation as cash capex; conservative nonoperating/CWIP value; and an economic NCI proxy tested over bounds. These are not company commitments. Valuation excludes unverified project subsidies/carbon receipts. No forecasts were used as historical factor evidence.\\n\\n### Unresolved evidence and falsification\\n\\n1. Actual October cash, bank utilization, facility renewals and restrictions, and cash fungibility across entities.\\n2. Comparable annual product volumes, realizations and raw-material yields, current customer/supplier concentration and tender dispatch versus allocation reconciliation.\\n3. Clean FY24 restated-to-comparative capital/cashflow reconciliation, long-run organic acquisition-capital bridge, and mature incremental project returns.\\n4. Recoverability and terms of related-party/other advances, aged/disputed receivables and doubtful supplier advances; inventory grade, saleability and NRV testing.\\n5. CBG land/title, complete permits, budget, EPC commitments, credible feedstock cost/yield, offtake and signed financing/subsidy eligibility and receipts.\\n6. Current promoter encumbrance, surveillance/circuit rules, executable quote depth and lot-constrained exit plan.\\n\\n### Update and quality log\\n\\nInitial v3.0 analysis with20October2026 information cutoff and19October market close. During review, parent-versus-group PAT, FY25 comparative EPS, overview EBITDA, FD-in-CFO classification, FY21 narrative endpoint, goodwill reclassification and source rounding were corrected explicitly. Cash modeling prohibits pre-entry equity, stops failed paths and reserves terminal operating liquidity before excess-cash valuation. This log records methodological corrections, not claimed changes in the company after cutoff. Research remains a hold until the listed evidence resolves; no pending evidence is treated as proven misconduct or as a neutral score.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"original_heading\"","355","379","1359","\"## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\"","1408","5737","\"## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\"","5775","15338","\"## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\"","15362","17353","\"## 4. Business Overview\\n\"","17384","20054","\"## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\"","20083","22327","\"## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\"","22370","24454","\"## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\"","24495","27152","\"## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\"","27178","36571","\"## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\"","36610","47871","\"## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\"","47910","49801","\"## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\"","49829","52532","\"## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\"","52555","55317","\"## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\"","55366","56570","\"## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\"","56615","63768","\"## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\"","\"filename\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"d717dce8c547821f4c15204c34cb5600d1482a0dacf57afe01ec6a4c0b9ee679\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"a7dc9130aadca11391e94c59129c1f1ad0078fea79cb3295ddf6d4661bc41bbe\"","\"Authored analytical table or source register, not a bundled original filing. Source-access qualifications remain in the research.\"","\"content\"","\"historical_inputs.json\"","\"1aeba512eddc96759b525e837cb0679889f482a568b436d473f4126fa7f8c2a9\"","\"c2f3c9e7dfc1e0aa70a15203c0d529124f3bfbaaa06c7c35dc59aeb431ba0a98\"","\"factor_evidence.json\"","\"625c4552036450b657b1fd7aa6c78081f36aca0e2389e8c51fe09581b8161080\"","\"a04d56e1374c5f35a9efb3f7c4f2a5a93fe64ca29ebb3edbd520d51a88eeaa22\"","\"audit_evidence.json\"","\"4f48c26324507ee6baa562bdc8e2d656dac9b632ecdc29ba38bb745d13e3a021\"","\"21c8e604957bbaf687bd6c87b4cab4827196ed710ec7c91b72214474fa9be8a2\"","\"source_register.json\"","\"29f539b3a4e5a9285ca6d6e7c890fc71f387ec223ad93a6f8141f5abf35038de\"","\"6f2e0996a7b771c6f1007ef3f84bee2059ebadeaae0c43d6d0b7d351b8514b6a\"","\"3aa29378a0bc458a635619e8f96320790f59d0d3015761268aaaf00b7848274c\"","\"585dfd0f1a541b46cecaac164e40633f6c6e3a744a587b7c897374f96984cea8\"","\"9a2c1a17355bdab3be59fd2f132808e38d77ca1ad552556757a60d1d60d4ed3d\"","\"f96cf92b49602dee5484da5724c583c92f216a0db3391bb5e271a6159d64668a\"","\"model_output.json\"","\"9ef935eaaf6bdda00e4c4ee65a741085aaf1d57204ea028276c8823a97c81cfb\"","\"2385bf1c7ae3a2ee62fb1d5145bdc55dc0b61be7be19d2540bb31706e8d9ca63\"","\"cash_event_ledger.csv\"","\"c0f99466e806f581bfa61d44a7515ece884394c76a65c1a95f3e213afd7235cb\"","\"Exact UTF-8 CSV text\"","\"6f23b89a9c8048fd516e836488ed616b4586508c5f896f0483ba51d36bde79a0\"","\"reader_score_po